// VYGENEROVANÉ z lang/en.csv príkazom node tools/build-lang.js — neupravuj, uprav CSV.
DLG_LOAD("en", "key,text\nmain.finale.1,\"Final language exam\"\nmain.finale.2,\"📝 Exam\"\nmain.finale.3,\"Language traps + equations · {0} questions\"\nmain.finale.4,\"Excellent! One last exam: <b>language traps</b>. Pick the correct wording — in the quantum world many mistakes are made with words, not with calculations.\"\nmain.finale.5,\"Level complete! Rating: <b>{0}</b> (mistakes: {1}, difficulty: {2}).<br>You will find new cards in the <b>Codex</b> (key C).\"\nmain.eqIntroLines.1,\"You are playing <b>∑ Equation Language (Experimental)</b>. Above the scene floats the <b>real equation</b> that holds right now — with values that change when you do something. It is a plane in the same 3D world: it tilts with the camera.\"\nmain.eqIntroLines.2,\"The symbols in the equations are drawn as small pictures — glyphs. Hover over one and its tooltip tells you what it means. You will find all of them under the 🔑 key in the top right.\"\nmain.eqIntroLines.3,\"Before every task I will first show you its <b>equation</b> (the ∑ card) and only then the picture.\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.0,\"Hi! Welcome to <b>Hilbert Island</b>. I am Spark — a little spark of light that will guide you around the island. (Don’t mix me up with the amplitude symbol — that one has a magnitude and a phase, I just glow.)\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.1,\"And you are <b>Little Psi</b> — the quantum state <b>ψ</b>. You are not a little ball with a position and a velocity. You are a <i>rule for predictions</i>: you tell what outcomes anyone who asks you something (measures you) will get.\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.2,\"See that golden hand turning around you? That is your <b>global phase</b>. It turns, but nobody in the world can measure it. Remember: <b>the global phase is unobservable, the relative phase is not</b>.\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.3,\"There are 8 portals around the island. Behind each one a mentor is waiting — Euler, Stern, Bloch, Feynman, Dirac, Rabi, Bell and Bohr. They will teach you the <b>language</b>, the <b>symbols</b> and the <b>right pictures</b> of the quantum world.\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.4,\"Above the island circles <b>Ketvarr</b>, the quantum dragon. Each mentor will teach you one <b>Word of Power</b>. Once you have all eight, climb <b>Dragon’s Peak</b> in the north and defeat him.\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.5,\"The goal is not to compute integrals. The goal is <b>intuition</b>: knowing what an amplitude is, what a probability is, what a measurement does and where classical analogies stop working.\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.6,\"Controls: <b>WASD</b> move, <b>mouse drag</b> camera, <b>E</b> enter/talk, <b>C</b> Codex of symbols, <b>M</b> map, <b>H</b> help. Start with portal <b>1</b>!\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.7,\"You are playing the <b>Equation Language (Experimental)</b> type, so the ring has an extra portal <b>0 — the Hall of Symbols</b>. It introduces the symbols you will meet in the equations and what they mean; I suggest starting there.\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.8,\"New to the quantum world? Switch the difficulty (top right) to <b>🫶 Layman</b> — I will explain everything in everyday words.\"\nmain.guideTalk.1.9,\"One more thing: the island is full of <b>classical misconceptions</b>. Fight them with spells <b>1–7</b> (Tab = target, right-click = attack), gather experience and money, shop at <b>Planck’s</b> by the fountain (B = bags). Talk to me again and I will give you your first quest.\"\nmain.guideTalk.2,\". Don’t forget: <i>amplitudes interfere, probabilities are only measured.</i>\"\nwow.QUESTS.1.0,\"The meadows beyond the portals are crawling with <b>Hidden Variables</b>. They whisper that the outcome was fixed in advance.\"\nwow.QUESTS.1.1,\"Bell proved that no local hidden variables can explain quantum correlations. Debunk <b>5</b> of them — and come back to me.\"\nwow.QUESTS.2,\"Well done. Remember: quantum probabilities are not just our ignorance.\"\nwow.QUESTS.3.0,\"Billiard-Ball Electrons roll around the island as if they had a definite path.\"\nwow.QUESTS.3.1,\"Between measurements a quantum object has no trajectory. Break <b>6</b> of them.\"\nwow.QUESTS.4,\"A path is a picture from classical physics. You have a better one: the state and its amplitudes.\"\nwow.QUESTS.5.0,\"The Little-Planet Atoms claim electrons orbit the nucleus like planets around the Sun.\"\nwow.QUESTS.5.1,\"Such an electron would radiate and fall into the nucleus within a hundredth of a billionth of a second. Bring down <b>4</b>.\"\nwow.QUESTS.6,\"An orbital is not a path but a distribution of amplitudes. Exactly.\"\nwow.QUESTS.7.0,\"Faster-than-Light Signals fly over the bridge, promising messages faster than light.\"\nwow.QUESTS.7.1,\"Entanglement gives correlations, not messages — Bob’s statistics don’t depend on Alice’s choice. Intercept <b>4</b>.\"\nwow.QUESTS.8,\"No-signalling holds. Relativity breathes a sigh of relief.\"\nwow.QUESTS.9.0,\"“Dead-and-alive” cats haunt the edge of the island.\"\nwow.QUESTS.9.1,\"A superposition is not “both at once” — the state gives probabilities, a measurement one outcome. Calm <b>4</b>.\"\nwow.QUESTS.10,\"Schrödinger would be pleased. His cat was a critique, not a recipe.\"\nwow.QUESTS.11.0,\"The Consciousness Cultists preach that the world collapses only when someone looks.\"\nwow.QUESTS.11.1,\"A measurement is a physical interaction; decoherence runs even in an empty lab. Disperse <b>5</b>.\"\nwow.QUESTS.12,\"The island is clean of misconceptions. You are a true quantum mage.\"\nl1.intro.1,\"Welcome to the <b>Complex Harbour</b>! I am Leonhard Euler. I introduced the letters <b>e</b> and <b>i</b> — and in quantum mechanics you will see them at every step.\"\nl1.intro.2,\"An analogy: <b>an amplitude is like the hand of a clock</b>. It has a <b>length</b> (magnitude) and an <b>angle</b> (phase). In the quantum world every possible outcome carries such a hand.\"\nl1.intro.3,\"The probability is the <b>square of the length</b> of the hand: |α|². It doesn’t care about the angle at all! So why does phase exist? You’ll find out in the third task. 😉\"\nl1.intro.4,\"Beneath you is the <b>complex plane</b>: horizontally <b>Re</b> (real part), vertically <b>Im</b> (imaginary part). The circle is the unit circle.\"\nl1.upd.1,\"Exactly! Notice: while you changed only the <b>phase</b>, the probability bar didn’t move. It changed only when you changed the <b>magnitude</b>.\"\nl1.upd.2.q,\"Two amplitudes: 0.7 and [[0.7·e<sup>iπ/2</sup> (= 0.7i)]]. Do they have the same probability?\"\nl1.upd.2.options.0,\"Yes, both 0.49\"\nl1.upd.2.options.1,\"No, the second has −0.49\"\nl1.upd.2.options.2,\"No, the second has 0.7\"\nl1.upd.2.why,\"[[|0.7i|² = 0.7i · (−0.7i) = 0.49]]. The phase is lost in |α|².\"\nl1.press.1.0,\"Done in {0} presses. <b>Multiplying by i = rotating by 90°</b>. Two rotations = 180°, so <b>[[i · i = i² = −1]]</b>. No magic, just geometry!\"\nl1.press.1.1,\"And one more thing: [[e<sup>iπ</sup> = −1]]. The phase π (a 180° rotation) is exactly the <b>minus sign</b> that distinguishes the states |+⟩ and |−⟩. You will meet them in the Bloch Observatory.\"\nl1.press.2.q,\"How many times must we multiply the number 1 by i to get to −i?\"\nl1.press.2.options.0,\"3 times (270°)\"\nl1.press.2.options.1,\"once\"\nl1.press.2.options.2,\"4 times\"\nl1.press.2.why,\"[[1 → i → −1 → −i]]. Four presses would bring us back to 1.\"\nl1.interference.1,\"Now the most important part. <b>Two paths</b> lead to the same outcome, each with its own amplitude A₁ and A₂ (length 0.5).\"\nl1.interference.2,\"The quantum rule: <b>first add the hands (amplitudes), only then square</b>: [[P = |A₁ + A₂|²]]. The classical rule for mutually exclusive alternatives would add probabilities: [[|A₁|² + |A₂|² = 0.5]] always.\"\nl1.interference.3,\"An analogy: two people push a swing. If they push <b>in rhythm</b>, the swing flies high. If they push <b>against each other</b>, it doesn’t move. What matters is the rhythm — the <b>relative phase</b>.\"\nl1.upd.3,\"Excellent! At phase [[π, P = 0]], even though each path alone would give ¼. <b>This is the core of quantum mechanics</b>: the phase is invisible in a single amplitude, but it decides when amplitudes meet.\"\nl1.upd.4,\"Quantum algorithms do exactly this: they arrange the phases so that wrong answers cancel and right ones are reinforced.\"\nl2.intro.1.0.who,\"Otto Stern\"\nl2.intro.1.0.text,\"Welcome to Frankfurt, 1922! Silver atoms fly out of this <b>furnace</b>. Each has one unpaired electron, and it behaves like a tiny magnet.\"\nl2.intro.1.1.who,\"Walther Gerlach\"\nl2.intro.1.1.text,\"We send the beam through an <b>inhomogeneous</b> magnetic field (the top pole is sharp, the bottom one flat). Depending on its orientation, the magnetic moment is deflected up or down.\"\nl2.intro.1.2.who,\"Otto Stern\"\nl2.intro.1.2.text,\"The classical picture: the little magnets point in random directions → a <b>continuous band</b> should appear on the screen. Let’s test it!\"\nl2.twoSpots.1.q,\"How many spots does the real (quantum) beam make on the screen?\"\nl2.twoSpots.1.options.0,\"two separate spots\"\nl2.twoSpots.1.options.1,\"a continuous band\"\nl2.twoSpots.1.options.2,\"one spot in the middle\"\nl2.twoSpots.1.why,\"Measuring the projection of a spin ½ along a chosen axis has only two outcomes: <b>+ħ/2</b> and <b>−ħ/2</b>.\"\nl2.twoSpots.2.0.who,\"Walther Gerlach\"\nl2.twoSpots.2.0.text,\"We call the upper spot <b>[[S<sub>z</sub> = +ħ/2]]</b> (state <b>[[|0⟩ ≡ |+z⟩]]</b>, “spin up”), the lower one <b>[[S<sub>z</sub> = −ħ/2]]</b> (state <b>[[|1⟩ ≡ |−z⟩]]</b>).\"\nl2.twoSpots.2.1.who,\"Otto Stern\"\nl2.twoSpots.2.1.text,\"Mind the language! The experiment <b>did not show how the electron spins</b>. It showed what outcomes a precisely specified measurement gives. And “spin ½” doesn’t mean half a turn — it is the name of a kind of quantum spin.\"\nl2.sequences.1.0.who,\"Otto Stern\"\nl2.sequences.1.0.text,\"Now you can chain up to <b>three magnets</b>, rotate them (z axis = 0°, x axis = 90°) and set a <b>filter</b> that lets only one beam through.\"\nl2.sequences.1.1.who,\"Walther Gerlach\"\nl2.sequences.1.1.text,\"Setup A: the first Z magnet lets only “+” through, the second magnet measures Z again. Setup B: put an X magnet (passing “+”) between them. Guess the result before you fire!\"\nl2.sequences.2.q,\"Why does setup [[B (Z+ → X+ → Z)]] give 50 : 50 again at the end?\"\nl2.sequences.2.options.0,\"The x measurement prepared a new state |+x⟩; in it the z outcome is uncertain.\"\nl2.sequences.2.options.1,\"The X magnet damaged the atoms.\"\nl2.sequences.2.options.2,\"The atoms had hidden values for all axes and the X magnet shuffled them.\"\nl2.sequences.2.why,\"Certainty in one basis does not mean certainty in another. <b>The measurement basis is part of the question.</b> Spin is not an arrow with predetermined values for x, y and z all at once.\"\nl2.predict.1,\"The atoms passed a “+z” filter. The second magnet is rotated {0}° from the z axis. What fraction goes to its upper spot?\"\nl2.predict.2,\"The probability is [[cos²({0}°/2) = {1}]]. On the Bloch sphere: (1 + cos {0}°)/2. The smaller the angle between the axes, the more certain the outcome.\"\nl2.predict.3,\"Otto Stern\"\nl2.predict.4,\"We measured {0} up (theory {1}). A single measurement always gives just +ħ/2 or −ħ/2, but the <b>statistics of many repetitions</b> reveal the probability. That is exactly how the Born rule is tested in the lab.\"\nl3.BLOCH_PUZZLES.1,\"<b>X</b> = a 180° rotation about the x axis. From the north pole to the south: quantum NOT, a “bit flip”. Notice: |0⟩ and |1⟩ are <b>perpendicular</b> in Hilbert space but <b>antipodal</b> on the Bloch sphere.\"\nl3.BLOCH_PUZZLES.2,\"<b>H</b> rotates about the axis between x and z → swaps the axes [[z ↔ x]]. Mind the language: H “creates a superposition” only from the point of view of the Z basis. Applied to |+⟩ it would “undo” one instead.\"\nl3.BLOCH_PUZZLES.3,\"Look at P(0) and P(1): <b>they didn’t change</b> (still ½ and ½)! Z changed only the <b>relative phase</b> by π. Indistinguishable in the Z basis, completely different states in the X basis.\"\nl3.BLOCH_PUZZLES.4,\"H brought you down to the equator [[(θ = π/2), S]] turned the phase by [[π/2 (φ = π/2)]]. Two angles θ and φ are enough to describe any pure qubit.\"\nl3.BLOCH_PUZZLES.5,\"[[H·Z·H = X]]. Different sequences of rotations can give the same overall rotation. Every gate is <b>unitary</b> = a rotation that preserves the length of the arrow.\"\nl3.BLOCH_PUZZLES.6,\"Look at the notation: [[Y|0⟩ = <b>i|1⟩</b>]]. The factor i is a <b>global phase</b> — the same for the whole state, impossible to measure. That’s why we write <b>[[i|1⟩ ∼ |1⟩]]</b> (“equal up to a global phase”). The arrow on the sphere is the same!\"\nl3.BLOCH_PUZZLES.7,\"E.g. [[H → |+⟩, S → |+i⟩, Z → |−i⟩]] (or H, Z, S). Relative phase [[φ = 3π/2]] — that is −i. Different paths over the sphere, the same target.\"\nl3.BLOCH_PUZZLES.8,\"[[T·T = S]]: two 45° rotations about z make 90°. The phase gates form a ladder: [[T² = S, S² = Z]].\"\nl3.intro.1.0,\"Grüezi! I am Felix Bloch. Thanks to NMR I showed that the state of a spin can be drawn as an <b>arrow in a ball</b>. Today we call it the <b>Bloch sphere</b>.\"\nl3.intro.1.1,\"An analogy: the Bloch sphere is a <b>globe of states</b>. North pole = |0⟩, south = |1⟩, the equator = equal superpositions. <b>Latitude</b> (the angle θ) sets the measurement probabilities in the Z basis, <b>longitude</b> (φ) is the relative phase.\"\nl3.intro.1.2,\"But careful: this is <b>not</b> a map of the laboratory, and the arrow is not the axis of a spinning ball! It is a picture of the amplitudes α and β.\"\nl3.intro.1.3,\"Gates are <b>rotations</b> of this ball. Solve {0} puzzles: bring the (red) arrow onto the golden target with a limited set of gates. You can rotate the ball with the mouse.\"\nl3.onAnimDone.1.q,\"The states |+⟩ and |−⟩ have the same [[P(0) = P(1) = ½]]. Are they the same state?\"\nl3.onAnimDone.1.options.0,\"No — they differ by a relative phase, and a measurement in the X basis tells them apart with certainty\"\nl3.onAnimDone.1.options.1,\"Yes — same probabilities = same state\"\nl3.onAnimDone.1.options.2,\"Yes, they differ only by a global phase\"\nl3.onAnimDone.1.why,\"Equal statistics in one basis do not mean the same state. They are even orthogonal!\"\nl3.measure.1.0,\"The last lesson: <b>measurement</b>. The arrow holds a lot of information (both θ and φ). How much of it do you get from one measurement?\"\nl3.measure.1.1,\"Click <b>Measure 1×</b> on the state |+⟩. Then <b>Measure 100 copies</b> — the same preparations, independent measurements.\"\nl3.checkMeasure.1.q,\"What did ONE measurement of the qubit give us?\"\nl3.checkMeasure.1.options.0,\"one bit (0 or 1), and the state changed to |0⟩ or |1⟩\"\nl3.checkMeasure.1.options.1,\"the values of α and β\"\nl3.checkMeasure.1.options.2,\"the angles θ and φ\"\nl3.checkMeasure.1.why,\"One measurement = one bit. You learn the probabilities only from the <b>statistics of many identically prepared copies</b>. And the phase φ only by measuring in another basis. (Try it: |+⟩ and |+i⟩ give the same statistics in the Z basis!)\"\nl3.lab.1,\"Finally, a free lab. Every pure state is a point (θ, φ), every gate is a rotation R<sub>n</sub>(α) about some axis n, and every measurement is the question “is the state along m?” — <b>[[P(+m) = (1 + r·m)/2]]</b>.\"\nl4.intro.1.0,\"Hey! I’m Dick Feynman. I once said the double-slit experiment contains <b>the only mystery</b> of quantum mechanics. Here is its qubit version.\"\nl4.intro.1.1,\"The track: the qubit starts in |0⟩, passes through gate <b>H</b>, the centre of the temple and a second gate <b>H</b>. At the end a detector measures it: 0 or 1.\"\nl4.intro.1.2,\"An analogy: <b>two paths</b> lead to the treasure. As long as nobody knows which one you took, their amplitudes can add up or cancel. Once someone <b>finds out</b> (measures), the interference disappears — only ordinary probabilities remain.\"\nl4.intro.1.3,\"At the back you see the <b>density matrix ρ</b> (rho) as bars: the two on the diagonal = probabilities (populations), the two off the diagonal = <b>coherences</b>, the “memory of the phase”.\"\nl4.pure.1.q,\"Qubit [[|0⟩ → H → H]] → measurement. What do you measure?\"\nl4.pure.1.options.0,\"always 0\"\nl4.pure.1.options.1,\"50 % : 50 %\"\nl4.pure.1.options.2,\"always 1\"\nl4.pure.1.why,\"[[H·H = I]]. The contributions to |1⟩ cancel destructively, those to |0⟩ add up constructively.\"\nl4.withMeasure.1.q,\"Now we put a Z-basis measurement in the centre and FORGET its result. What do you measure at the end?\"\nl4.withMeasure.1.options.0,\"50 % : 50 %\"\nl4.withMeasure.1.options.1,\"always 0\"\nl4.withMeasure.1.options.2,\"always 1\"\nl4.withMeasure.1.why,\"The measurement destroys the coherence (off-diagonal elements of ρ). What remains is the mixture [[½|0⟩⟨0| + ½|1⟩⟨1| = I/2]] — and the second H doesn’t change it.\"\nl4.deco.1.0,\"A measurement is the extreme case. In a real chip the qubit slowly “leaks” into the environment: <b>decoherence</b>. It is as if the environment partly measured the phase.\"\nl4.deco.1.1,\"Use the slider to set the decoherence strength <b>p</b> (0 = nothing, 1 = a full measurement). Task: find p for which [[P(0) = <b>{0}</b>]], and send at least {1} qubits.\"\nl4.deco.1.2,\"Also new: a <b>phase shifter φ</b> in the centre of the temple (a rotation about the z axis). It decides whether the paths meet “in rhythm” — interference fringes: [[P(0) = (1 + (1 − p)·cos φ)/2]].\"\nl4.check.1,\"See? Always 0. Look at ρ along the way: after the first H <b>all four bars are equally tall</b> — the off-diagonal coherences carry the phase information, and the second H turns it into certainty.\"\nl4.check.2.0,\"After the measurement the off-diagonal bars <b>vanished</b>. The diagonal is the same as for the superposition (½, ½), so a <b>measurement in the Z basis cannot tell them apart</b>. The difference shows only in the next operation.\"\nl4.check.2.1,\"On the Bloch sphere: the arrow shrank to the <b>centre</b> (the maximally mixed state I/2). The second H rotates the ball, but a point at the centre does not move under rotation.\"\nl4.check.3.0,\"Exactly: [[p = {0}]] shrinks the coherences to {1} and [[P(0) = (1 + (1 − p)·cos φ)/2 = {2}]]. The arrow is shorter than 1 → a <b>mixed state</b> inside the ball.\"\nl4.check.3.1,\"Mathematically, the second gate acts on the density matrix as <b>HρH†</b> (the dagger † = Hermitian conjugate). For the mixture I/2 you get I/2 again — nothing changes.\"\nl4.check.3.2,\"The lesson for quantum computers: <b>coherence is the fuel</b> of interference. Lose it and you lose the advantage.\"\nl5.DIRAC_TASKS.1,\"⟨a|ψ⟩ — the bra “asks the question” (is it state a?), the ket is the answer. Together they give a <b>number</b>: the amplitude. Bra-ket = “bracket”.\"\nl5.DIRAC_TASKS.2,\"|⟨a|ψ⟩|² — the Born rule. The amplitude is theory, the probability is experiment.\"\nl5.DIRAC_TASKS.3,\"|ψ⟩⟨ψ| — column times row = a <b>matrix</b>. The same symbols as in ⟨ψ|ψ⟩, the opposite order, a completely different object!\"\nl5.DIRAC_TASKS.4,\"⟨ψ|Â|ψ⟩ — the average of many measurements. Careful: it need not be a value you can get in a single run!\"\nl5.DIRAC_TASKS.5,\"Ĥ|ψ⟩ — the operator “processes” a ket and returns a ket. An operator acts to the right, on a ket.\"\nl5.DIRAC_TASKS.6,\"⟨φ|Â|ψ⟩ — a complex number, an element of the matrix. Read from the right: Â acts on ψ, the result is projected onto φ.\"\nl5.DIRAC_TASKS.7,\"|ψ⟩ ⊗ |φ⟩ — the tensor product. For two qubits [[|0⟩ ⊗ |1⟩ = |01⟩]]. Dimensions multiply: 2 × 2 = 4 amplitudes.\"\nl5.intro.1.0,\"Dirac.\"\nl5.intro.1.1.who,\"Librarian Ket\"\nl5.intro.1.1.text,\"(whispering) Professor Dirac is famously taciturn. His colleagues supposedly introduced the unit <b>1 dirac = one word per hour</b>. Allow me to interpret.\"\nl5.intro.1.2.who,\"Librarian Ket\"\nl5.intro.1.2.text,\"The professor invented the <b>bra-ket notation</b> — the grammar of quantum mechanics. Every expression has a <b>type</b>, just as a word in a language has a part of speech: <b>ket |ψ⟩</b> = state (column), <b>bra ⟨ψ|</b> = question (row), <b>Â</b> = operator (machine).\"\nl5.intro.1.3.who,\"Librarian Ket\"\nl5.intro.1.3.text,\"An analogy: a ket is a <b>letter</b>, a bra is an <b>addressed envelope</b>. Put the letter into the envelope — ⟨a|ψ⟩ — and you get a <b>number</b> (a stamp, an amplitude). Put them the other way round, |ψ⟩⟨a|, and you get a <b>machine</b> (an operator) that turns letters into other letters.\"\nl5.intro.1.4.who,\"Librarian Ket\"\nl5.intro.1.4.text,\"On the table (3D) you will see shapes: <b>arrow</b> = ket, <b>plank</b> = bra, <b>cube</b> = operator, <b>coin</b> = number, <b>bar</b> = probability. Put tokens together and learn what comes out of what.\"\nl5.intro.1.5,\"Correct.\"\nl5.verify.1,\"Librarian Ket\"\nl5.verify.2.q,\"Which expression is an OPERATOR?\"\nl5.verify.2.options.0,\"|ψ⟩⟨φ|\"\nl5.verify.2.options.1,\"⟨φ|ψ⟩\"\nl5.verify.2.options.2,\"⟨ψ|Â|ψ⟩\"\nl5.verify.2.why,\"Ket-bra = operator; bra-ket = number; ⟨ψ|Â|ψ⟩ = number (expectation value).\"\nl5.after.1.0,\"Good.\"\nl5.after.1.1.who,\"Librarian Ket\"\nl5.after.1.1.text,\"(From him that is almost an ode!) The main idea: <b>|ψ⟩ is an abstract state</b>, and [[ψ(x) = ⟨x|ψ⟩]] is just its “coordinate” in the position basis — just like [[c<sub>n</sub> = ⟨n|ψ⟩]] in a discrete basis.\"\nl6.intro.1.0,\"Shalom! I am I. I. Rabi. In 1938 I taught atoms to “listen to the radio” — that is <b>magnetic resonance</b>. From it came NMR, MRI and atomic clocks.\"\nl6.intro.1.1.who,\"Pieter Zeeman\"\nl6.intro.1.1.text,\"Allow me to add: in a static field <b>B₀</b> the energy of a spin ½ splits into <b>two levels</b> (the Zeeman effect). Those two levels are our qubit.\"\nl6.intro.1.2,\"On the left is the NMR magnet with a sample (e.g. dimethyl phosphite molecules in a SpinQ). On the right is the Bloch sphere of the nuclear spin. Down in the panel there is a plot: <b>P(|1⟩)</b> and the <b>NMR signal</b>.\"\nl6.intro.1.3,\"An analogy: an RF pulse is like <b>pushing a swing</b>: if you push in the right rhythm (<b>resonance</b>), the swing goes all the way. Out of rhythm it only wobbles a bit.\"\nl6.precession.1.q,\"In the laboratory frame the arrow circled around the z axis (precession). Did the probability P(|1⟩) change meanwhile?\"\nl6.precession.1.options.0,\"No — precession changes only the relative phase φ, not θ\"\nl6.precession.1.options.1,\"Yes, it oscillated\"\nl6.precession.1.options.2,\"Yes, it kept growing\"\nl6.precession.1.why,\"Look at the plot: the green line is flat, only the orange signal oscillates. Precession ≠ gyroscope: it is the evolution of the phase between |0⟩ and |1⟩.\"\nl6.precession.2,\"In the <b>rotating frame</b> we turn together with the spin (at the Larmor frequency), so the fast precession “stops”. It is a mathematical trick — the laboratory does not rotate. From now on we will work in this frame.\"\nl6.piPulse.1.0,\"That is a <b>π pulse</b>: a 180° rotation — like the X gate. The pulse area Ω<sub>R</sub>·t is the <b>rotation angle</b>, not a time in seconds.\"\nl6.piPulse.1.1,\"If you made the pulse longer, the state would come back: those are <b>Rabi oscillations</b>. It is the probability that oscillates, not an electron between two places!\"\nl6.halfPulse.1,\"A <b>π/2 pulse</b> — an equal superposition. In NMR it is the basic step of almost every experiment.\"\nl6.tuning.1,\"Someone has moved the frequency of the RF generator. Off resonance the spin rotates about a <b>tilted axis</b> and never fully flips. Find the rhythm of the swing!\"\nl6.tuning.2,\"Resonance found! This is exactly how the frequency of a nucleus is found in NMR. The frequency difference (detuning) tilts the rotation axis.\"\nl6.t2.1,\"Real spins feel their neighbours and field inhomogeneities. Each molecule precesses a little differently and the phases drift apart: <b>T₂ (dephasing)</b>. The slower return of populations to the thermal state is <b>T₁</b>.\"\nl6.update.1.0,\"The arrow has shrunk towards the axis — a <b>mixed state</b> (|r| < 1). Meanwhile the orange NMR signal faded: that is the <b>FID</b> (free induction decay).\"\nl6.update.1.1,\"In NMR (e.g. SpinQ) we do not measure a single spin but an <b>ensemble</b> of ~10²⁰ molecules. The signal is the <b>expectation value</b> of the transverse magnetisation, not individual 0/1 outcomes.\"\nl7.intro.1.0,\"Good day, I am John Bell from CERN. Welcome to the <b>bridge of entanglement</b>. On the left stands <b>Alice</b> with her qubit, on the right <b>Bob</b>.\"\nl7.intro.1.1.text,\"And I am Einstein. In 1935, with Podolsky and Rosen, we argued that quantum mechanics is <b>incomplete</b>. If I can predict something at a distance with certainty, it must be an “element of reality” beforehand! No <i>spukhafte Fernwirkung</i> — spooky action at a distance.\"\nl7.intro.1.2,\"In 1964 I showed how this dispute can be <b>settled by experiment</b>. But first you have to make an entangled pair.\"\nl7.after.1.0,\"Excellent! Now look at the Bloch spheres: <b>both arrows vanished into the centre</b>.\"\nl7.after.1.1,\"The whole |Φ⁺⟩ is a pure state, but <b>neither qubit on its own has a state of its own</b> — each is maximally mixed. The information is neither “in Alice” nor “in Bob”, it is in the <b>correlations</b>. That is entanglement: the whole is more than the sum of its parts.\"\nl7.measurePairs.1.0.text,\"With Z–Z the outcomes are <b>always the same</b>! I told you: the values must have been fixed in advance, like two gloves in two boxes.\"\nl7.measurePairs.1.1,\"Perhaps. But look at Bob: whether Alice measures Z or X, Bob sees <b>50 : 50 every time</b>. This way Alice <b>cannot send him a message</b> — they see the correlations only after comparing results over an ordinary (classical, slower) channel.\"\nl7.measurePairs.1.2,\"And a game will decide about the gloves. Let’s go!\"\nl7.chsh.1.0,\"The rules of the <b>CHSH game</b>: the referee sends Alice a random bit x and Bob a random bit y. Without communicating, they answer with bits a, b.\"\nl7.chsh.1.1,\"They win if <b>a ⊕ [[b = x · y]]</b>: so they should answer <b>the same</b>, except when [[x = y = 1]], when they should answer <b>differently</b>.\"\nl7.chsh.1.2.text,\"If they have “gloves” — answers agreed in advance (local hidden variables) — they never exceed <b>75 %</b>. That is Bell’s inequality.\"\nl7.chsh.1.3,\"With an entangled pair, Alice picks the measurement angle a₀ or a₁ depending on x, and Bob picks b₀ or b₁ depending on y. Try to find angles that beat 75 %!\"\nl7.play.1.0,\"{0}! The theoretical maximum is [[cos²(π/8) ≈ <b>85 %</b>]]. <b>No gloves can do this</b>.\"\nl7.play.1.1.text,\"Hmm... So either I give up locality, or the idea that values exist before the measurement.\"\nl7.play.1.2,\"Exactly. <b>Bohm</b> chooses non-locality (the pilot wave), the operational approach (Bohr) gives up pre-existing values. And note: you still haven’t sent each other a <b>message</b>. Aspect, Clauser and Zeilinger received the 2022 Nobel Prize for the experimental confirmation.\"\nl8.intro.1.0,\"Velkommen! I am Niels Bohr. My coat of arms bears the yin-yang and the motto <i>Contraria sunt complementa</i> — opposites are complementary.\"\nl8.intro.1.1,\"A physicist must be able to calculate. But he must also be able to <b>speak</b> — and not speak nonsense. In this hall stand the thinkers your lectures mention.\"\nl8.intro.1.2,\"<b>Complementarity</b>: a quantum object cannot be described by a single classical picture. Wave and particle are both precise descriptions, each within its own experimental arrangement. This is not relativism!\"\nl8.intro.1.3,\"Talk to all six of them. Each will ask you a question.\"\nl8.sorting.1.0,\"Excellent. The lecture warns you: don’t mix up <b>three questions</b> — <b>ontology</b> (What exists?), <b>epistemology</b> (What can we know about it?), <b>phenomenology</b> (How does the phenomenon appear to us?).\"\nl8.sorting.1.1,\"Classify the following sentences.\"\nl8.sorting.2.0.who,\"Niels Bohr\"\nl8.sorting.2.0.q,\"“Is the wave function ψ physical reality, or just a tool for prediction?” — What kind of question is this?\"\nl8.sorting.2.0.options.0,\"ontological\"\nl8.sorting.2.0.options.1,\"epistemological\"\nl8.sorting.2.0.options.2,\"phenomenological\"\nl8.sorting.2.0.why,\"It asks what exists. Neither the Schrödinger equation nor experiments settle this dispute by themselves.\"\nl8.sorting.2.1.who,\"Niels Bohr\"\nl8.sorting.2.1.q,\"“What can we know at the same time about the position and momentum of an electron?”\"\nl8.sorting.2.1.options.0,\"epistemological\"\nl8.sorting.2.1.options.1,\"ontological\"\nl8.sorting.2.1.options.2,\"phenomenological\"\nl8.sorting.2.1.why,\"A question about the limits of knowledge.\"\nl8.sorting.2.2.who,\"Niels Bohr\"\nl8.sorting.2.2.q,\"“How does spin appear to us in the Stern–Gerlach experiment — two spots on the screen?”\"\nl8.sorting.2.2.options.0,\"phenomenological\"\nl8.sorting.2.2.options.1,\"ontological\"\nl8.sorting.2.2.options.2,\"epistemological\"\nl8.sorting.2.2.why,\"A question about how a phenomenon appears in experience/experiment.\"\nl8.sorting.2.3.who,\"Niels Bohr\"\nl8.sorting.2.3.q,\"Which of these is NOT one of the “four questions” to ask about every new quantum concept?\"\nl8.sorting.2.3.options.0,\"Which observer is right?\"\nl8.sorting.2.3.options.1,\"What system have we prepared?\"\nl8.sorting.2.3.options.2,\"In what experiment do the properties show up?\"\nl8.sorting.2.3.options.3,\"What does this description still NOT imply?\"\nl8.sorting.2.3.why,\"The four questions: the system, the equation and assumptions, the experiment, and what does (not) follow from the description.\"\nl8.sorting.3,\"“There is no need to stop calculating the world. What we must not forget is what kind of world it is that we are calculating.” — so ends the lecture on the language of quantum mechanics.\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.0.q,\"The dragon has P(hit) = 70 %. You struck and missed. What happened?\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.0.options.0,\"The measurement gave the less likely outcome — 30 % happens too; the ward collapsed onto the opposite pole\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.0.options.1,\"The probability was computed wrongly\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.0.options.2,\"The dragon noticed you measuring and changed the angle\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.0.why,\"The Born rule gives probabilities, not certainties. After a measurement the state is in one of the two outcomes.\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.1.q,\"Why did the “Phase Hiss” (a rotation about z) not hurt you while you stood at |1⟩?\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.1.options.0,\"The poles are eigenstates of Z — a rotation about z changes them only by a global phase\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.1.options.1,\"Because the dragon missed\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.1.options.2,\"Because |1⟩ is the zero vector\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.1.why,\"[[Rz(φ)|1⟩ = e<sup>iφ/2</sup>|1⟩]] — a global phase is unobservable.\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.2.q,\"The Fog of Decoherence shrank the ward’s arrow to length 0.5. What is the highest P(hit) you can reach with gates alone?\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.2.options.0,\"75 % — rotations do not lengthen the arrow: [[P ≤ (1 + |r|)/2]]\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.2.options.1,\"100 % — you just need the right gate\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.2.options.2,\"50 %\"\nl9.DRAGON_TRAPS.1.2.why,\"Unitary gates are rotations; a mixed state stays mixed. Only a measurement (collapse) helps — or not letting the fog catch you on the equator.\"\nl9.intro.1.0,\"So you have passed through all eight portals. I am Erwin Schrödinger — yes, the one with the cat. On this mountain peak nests <b>Ketvarr</b>, the quantum dragon.\"\nl9.intro.1.1,\"His scales are guarded by <b>a ward that is a qubit</b>. You see it on the right as a Bloch ball. The golden arrow <b>n</b> is his weak spot.\"\nl9.intro.1.2,\"Your sword is <b>measurement</b>. A strike hits with probability <b>[[P = (1 + r·n)/2]]</b> — the closer the ward’s arrow is to the golden one, the surer the hit. And beware: after a strike the ward <b>collapses</b> onto one of the poles.\"\nl9.intro.1.3,\"Don’t say the dragon is “in the sky and on the ground at once”. Say it precisely: he is in a state that gives <b>P(hit)</b>. The two translucent dragons only show the probabilities of the outcomes.\"\nl9.intro.1.4,\"The fight is <b>turn-based</b>: you shout one Word of Power (a gate), then the dragon makes an <b>announced move</b> and breathes fire. Only a strike with <b>[[P ≥ {0}]]</b> wounds Ketvarr — that is the metric you must hit.\"\nl9.lines.1.0.0,\"🐉 <b>Ketvarr:</b> “A little state ψ wants to measure a dragon? My scales are made of stone and phases!”\"\nl9.lines.1.0.1,\"Schrödinger: His moves in round 1 are mostly <b>rotations about z</b>. Those don’t move the poles… and now and then a <b>Wing Flip (X)</b>. Watch the announced move and the numbers on the buttons — they show P(hit) after the dragon’s move.\"\nl9.lines.1.1.0,\"🐉 <b>Ketvarr:</b> “Fog! Let your coherence dissolve!”\"\nl9.lines.1.1.1,\"Schrödinger: Now he breathes the <b>Fog of Decoherence</b>, which shrinks the arrow on the equator. You have learned a new word: <b>RABI-RA</b> — a pulse with an adjustable angle θ. Don’t linger on the equator!\"\nl9.lines.1.2.0,\"🐉 <b>Ketvarr:</b> “My heart is not where you seek it!”\"\nl9.lines.1.2.1,\"Schrödinger: The weak spot <b>n</b> is now tilted, and the dragon moves it. The pulse also has an <b>axis φ</b> in the equatorial plane. Aim so that after the dragon’s move P ≥ the threshold.\"\nl9.startPhase.1,\"Ketvarr\"\nl9.msg.1.0.0,\"Ketvarr roars and his stone scales crack! “The poles… you stood on the poles, where my phase can do nothing!”\"\nl9.msg.1.0.1,\"Well done. Rotations about z change only the phase — at the poles |0⟩, |1⟩ they are harmless.\"\nl9.msg.1.1.0,\"The fog parts. “My fog… you did not linger on the equator!”\"\nl9.msg.1.1.1,\"Exactly. Decoherence destroys coherences — the arrow on the equator. A quick pulse and a measurement beat it.\"\nl9.msg.1.2.0,\"Ketvarr falls into the snow. “You aimed at my heart… even though it moved.”\"\nl9.msg.1.2.1,\"This is quantum control in miniature: predict the evolution and tune the pulse so that the outcome has a high probability.\"\nl9.phaseWon.1,\"Ketvarr\"\nl9.defeated.1,\"Ketvarr\"\nl9.defeated.2,\"“Ashes to ashes, amplitude to zero!”\"\nl9.defeated.3,\"The fire overwhelmed you. No matter — the round starts over. Tip: look at the numbers on the buttons and the dragon’s announced move before you shout.\"\nl9.finale.1,\"Ketvarr is defeated. Three more questions — I want to know whether you understand <b>why</b> you won.\"\nl9.finale.2,\"Ketvarr\"\nl9.finale.3,\"“Very well, little state. Nobody has ever measured me so precisely. I will fly to the northern peak and guard the island — against bad analogies.”\"\nl9.finale.4,\"🏆 <b>Hilbert Island is saved!</b> Battle rating: <b>{0}</b> (mistakes: {1}).<br>Remember: amplitudes interfere, probabilities are measured, and the dragon is in a state — not “in two places at once”.\"\nlayman.LAYMAN_INTRO.1.0,\"Hi! I am <b>Spark</b>, your guide. I will explain everything in <b>everyday words</b>.\"\nlayman.LAYMAN_INTRO.1.1,\"Ordinary computers use <b>bits</b>: each is 0 or 1. Quantum computers use <b>qubits</b>: until you look at one, a qubit can be <b>a blend of 0 and 1</b>.\"\nlayman.LAYMAN_INTRO.1.2,\"You are <b>Little Psi (ψ)</b> — the state of a qubit. You are not a little ball but <b>a recipe for chances</b>: you say what answers anyone who looks at you will get.\"\nlayman.LAYMAN_INTRO.1.3,\"The trick of quantum computers: every possible answer carries <b>a little arrow</b>. Arrows can <b>cancel each other out</b>. A good quantum program makes the wrong answers cancel and the right one grow.\"\nlayman.LAYMAN_INTRO.1.4,\"Above the island circles <b>Ketvarr, the quantum dragon</b>. Each mentor teaches you one Word of Power; with all eight you will defeat him on Dragon’s Peak.\"\nlayman.LAYMAN_INTRO.1.5,\"There are 8 portals on the island, each with a mentor. Before every task I will first <b>explain it in plain words</b>. Technical words get <b>a translation in brackets</b> and a simple explanation when you hover them.\"\nlayman.LAYMAN_INTRO.1.6,\"Controls: <b>WASD</b> move, <b>mouse drag</b> camera, <b>E</b> enter/talk, <b>M</b> map. Start with portal <b>1</b>!\"\nlayman.LAYMAN_INTRO.1.7,\"Once you feel more confident, you can switch to a harder difficulty with more technical language at any time (top right).\"\nlayman.1.intro.0,\"In a quantum computer <b>every possible answer carries a little arrow</b> (like a clock hand).\"\nlayman.1.intro.1,\"<b>Long arrow = a likely answer.</b> Short arrow = an unlikely one. The arrow’s direction (its “phase”) is like timing — it only matters when arrows meet.\"\nlayman.1.setHand.0,\"Simple task: <b>drag the arrow onto the golden mark</b> — the right length and the right direction. That’s all “setting an amplitude” means.\"\nlayman.1.timesI.0,\"The “× i” button just <b>turns the arrow a quarter turn</b> (90°) and keeps its length. Two quarter turns = the arrow points backwards.\"\nlayman.1.interference.0,\"<b>Two routes</b> lead to the same answer, and each brings its own arrow. The arrows <b>add up tip to tail</b>.\"\nlayman.1.interference.1,\"Same direction → they team up (a surer answer). Opposite directions → <b>they cancel</b> (that answer can’t happen). This is exactly how quantum computers win: <b>wrong answers cancel, the right one grows</b>.\"\nlayman.2.intro.0,\"Atoms behave like <b>tiny magnets</b>. We send them through a big magnet and look where they land.\"\nlayman.2.intro.1,\"Everyday experience says: a smeared stripe. Reality says: <b>only two spots — “up” or “down”</b>. That “always one of two answers” property is exactly a <b>qubit</b> (a quantum bit).\"\nlayman.2.twoSpots.0,\"First fire in the <b>“classical” model</b> (how it would work by everyday intuition), then in <b>reality</b>. Compare: a stripe vs. two dots.\"\nlayman.2.sequences.0,\"A filter = <b>we ask a question and keep only one answer</b>. Ask “up/down?”, then “left/right?”, then “up/down?” again.\"\nlayman.2.sequences.1,\"Surprise: the middle question <b>wipes out</b> the first answer. In the quantum world <b>asking a question changes what you ask about</b>.\"\nlayman.2.predict.0,\"A tilted magnet = <b>a slightly different question</b>. The more it’s tilted, the more the answer becomes a coin toss (50/50). Guess first, then fire.\"\nlayman.3.intro.0,\"Picture a qubit as <b>an arrow inside a ball</b>. North pole = <b>0</b>, south pole = <b>1</b>, anywhere else = <b>a blend of 0 and 1</b>.\"\nlayman.3.intro.1,\"<b>Gates</b> (the operations of a quantum computer) just <b>turn this arrow</b>. A quantum program = a sequence of turns.\"\nlayman.3.puzzles.0,\"Each puzzle: get the arrow to the target with as few buttons as possible. <b>X</b> swaps top ↔ bottom, <b>H</b> swaps the north pole with a point on the equator, <b>Z</b> turns the arrow half a turn around the vertical axis.\"\nlayman.3.measure.0,\"Measuring = asking the qubit <b>“are you 0 or 1?”</b>. An arrow on the equator answers randomly, 50/50. The arrow then jumps to the pole of the answer.\"\nlayman.3.measure.1,\"One try tells you almost nothing; <b>a hundred tries show the pattern</b>. Real quantum computers also run the same program many times.\"\nlayman.3.lab.0,\"Free play: point the arrow anywhere, turn it and measure in any direction. <b>Nothing can go wrong here.</b>\"\nlayman.4.intro.0,\"There are two kinds of “not sure”: <b>a genuine blend</b> that can still interfere (superposition), and <b>a coin someone already tossed</b> but hasn’t shown you (a mixture).\"\nlayman.4.intro.1,\"In a single measurement they look the same. The difference shows when you do one more step before measuring.\"\nlayman.4.pure.0,\"<b>H twice in a row</b> always brings the qubit back to 0 — the two routes to the answer 1 cancel each other. That’s interference at work.\"\nlayman.4.withMeasure.0,\"<b>Peek in the middle</b> and the magic is gone: suddenly it’s 50/50 at the end. Looking (or any leak of information) destroys the interference.\"\nlayman.4.withMeasure.1,\"The ρ bars off the diagonal are <b>“the blend is still alive” indicators</b>. Watch them go dark.\"\nlayman.4.deco.0,\"Decoherence = <b>the surroundings peeking a little</b>. More noise → less interference. It is <b>enemy no. 1 of real quantum computers</b> — that’s why they are cooled almost to absolute zero.\"\nlayman.5.intro.0,\"Dirac’s notation is just <b>shorthand</b>. |ψ⟩ (a “ket”) = <b>a state</b>, like a noun. ⟨φ| (a “bra”) = <b>a question</b> you ask.\"\nlayman.5.intro.1,\"⟨φ|ψ⟩ = <b>the answer to the question</b>: a single number (an arrow). Its length squared = <b>the chance</b>.\"\nlayman.5.tasks.0,\"Build expressions from tiles like LEGO. Always ask: is the result <b>a state</b>, <b>a number</b>, or <b>a machine that changes states</b>?\"\nlayman.6.intro.0,\"NMR is the technique behind <b>the MRI scanner in a hospital</b> — and behind <b>the first quantum computers</b>, too.\"\nlayman.6.intro.1,\"A strong magnet gives every atomic nucleus <b>two energy levels (= 0 and 1)</b>. Radio pulses of the right tone switch it between them.\"\nlayman.6.precession.0,\"In the magnet the arrow <b>spins like a top</b>. From the lab it looks busy; from <b>a merry-go-round turning with it</b> (the rotating frame) it stands still.\"\nlayman.6.precession.1,\"While it spins, the chances of 0 and 1 <b>don’t change</b>.\"\nlayman.6.piPulse.0,\"A π pulse = a radio pulse just long enough to <b>flip 0 into 1</b>. It is the quantum <b>NOT</b> gate.\"\nlayman.6.halfPulse.0,\"A pulse half as long stops <b>halfway</b>: a 50/50 blend. That’s how superposition is made on real hardware.\"\nlayman.6.tuning.0,\"Like <b>tuning a radio</b>: the wrong frequency and the flip doesn’t work. Find the right station.\"\nlayman.6.t2.0,\"Leave the blend alone and it <b>fades</b> — the nuclei drift out of step. T₂ = <b>how long the qubit “remembers”</b>. A quantum computation must finish before that.\"\nlayman.7.intro.0,\"Entanglement: <b>two qubits share one joint state</b>. Their results are linked — always equal, for example — even when they are far apart.\"\nlayman.7.intro.1,\"Einstein called it “spooky”. Bell figured out how to <b>test it by experiment</b>.\"\nlayman.7.build.0,\"Recipe: <b>H on the first qubit</b> (make it a 50/50 blend), then <b>CNOT</b> (flip the second one if the first is 1). Done — they’re entangled.\"\nlayman.7.noSignal.0,\"Bob on his own always sees <b>a fair coin</b>, whatever Alice does. So entanglement <b>cannot send a message</b> faster than light — the link only shows up when they compare notes.\"\nlayman.7.chsh.0,\"A cooperation game: Alice and Bob can’t talk, but they can share entangled pairs. With ordinary strategies they win <b>at most 75 %</b> of rounds, with entanglement <b>about 85 %</b>.\"\nlayman.7.chsh.1,\"Tilt the measurement angles until you beat 75 %.\"\nlayman.9.intro.0,\"The finale! The dragon Ketvarr has <b>a ward that is a qubit</b> — the arrow in the ball on the right. Your sword is <b>measurement</b>: you strike and either hit or miss.\"\nlayman.9.intro.1,\"The chance of a hit grows the <b>closer the ward’s arrow points to the golden arrow</b>. It’s turn-based: you say one word (a turn of the arrow), then the dragon makes one move, which he <b>announces in advance</b>.\"\nlayman.9.phase1.0,\"The numbers on the buttons show <b>the chance of a hit next turn</b> — already including the dragon’s move. Just pick whatever is green, then strike.\"\nlayman.9.phase2.0,\"Now the dragon blows <b>fog</b> that shortens the arrow on the equator — a shorter arrow = worse aim. At the poles the fog does no harm. The new slider θ sets how far you turn the arrow.\"\nlayman.9.phase3.0,\"The golden arrow (the dragon’s heart) is tilted and the dragon moves it. Adjust the θ and φ sliders until the pulse button turns green — then shout it and strike next turn.\"\nlayman.8.intro.0,\"No more knobs — this level is about <b>what the theory means</b>. Physicists agree on the maths and the predictions but still argue about what is “really” going on.\"\nlayman.8.statues.0,\"Chat with six thinkers. Each sees it differently, and <b>you don’t have to pick a winner</b>.\"\nlayman.8.sorting.0,\"Sort questions into three piles: <b>what exists?</b> (being), <b>what can we know?</b> (knowing), <b>what do we see and experience?</b> (experience).\"\nlayman.finale.0,\"The final quiz tests <b>words, not sums</b>. Tip: <b>chance = arrow length squared</b>; arrows add up, chances don’t. The trap is usually an everyday word used in the wrong place.\"\ntheory.1.core,\"An amplitude is a complex number in polar form \n[[<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i> = |<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>|·e<sup>iφ</sup> = |<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>|(cos φ + i sin φ)]]\nThe Born rule: \n[[P = |<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>|² = <i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>·<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>* = (Re <i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>)² + (Im <i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>)²]]\nThe phase φ of a single amplitude is lost in P; it only shows up when amplitudes are added.\"\ntheory.1.setHand,\"Multiplying by a phase factor keeps the magnitude: \n[[|e<sup>iφ</sup><i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>| = |e<sup>iφ</sup>|·|<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>| = |<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>|]]\nCartesian form of the target: [[Re = |<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>| cos φ, Im = |<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>| sin φ]]. For 0.7·e<sup>i3π/4</sup>: [[Re ≈ −0.49, Im ≈ 0.49]].\"\ntheory.1.timesI,\"\n[[i = e<sup>iπ/2</sup> ⇒ i·z = |z|·e<sup>i(φ + π/2)</sup>]]\nPowers of i are points on the unit circle: [[i⁰ = 1, i¹ = i, i² = −1, i³ = −i, i⁴ = 1]]. Euler’s identity \n[[e<sup>iπ</sup> + 1 = 0]]\nlinks five fundamental constants.\"\ntheory.1.interference,\"For two indistinguishable paths: \n[[P = |A₁ + A₂|² = |A₁|² + |A₂|² + 2 Re(A₁A₂*)]]\nThe last term is the <b>interference</b> term. For [[|A₁| = |A₂| = ½]] and relative phase Δφ: \n[[P = ½(1 + cos Δφ) = cos²(Δφ/2)]]\nClassically (distinguishable paths) only [[|A₁|² + |A₂|² = ½]] remains.\"\ntheory.2.core,\"A spin ½ in a magnetic field: energy \n[[E = −μ·B, &nbsp; F<sub>z</sub> = μ<sub>z</sub> ∂B<sub>z</sub>/∂z]]\nA force appears only in an <b>inhomogeneous</b> field. Quantum mechanically S<sub>z</sub> has only two values: \n[[S<sub>z</sub> = ±ħ/2, &nbsp; ħ = h/2π ≈ 1.055·10⁻³⁴ J·s]]\n\"\ntheory.2.twoSpots,\"A classical randomly oriented magnet would have [[μ<sub>z</sub> = μ cos ϑ]] with cos ϑ uniform in [−1, 1] → a continuous band. An unpolarised beam is, quantum mechanically, \n[[ρ = ½|0⟩⟨0| + ½|1⟩⟨1| = I/2]]\n— the centre of the Bloch sphere, giving 50 : 50 along <i>every</i> axis.\"\ntheory.2.sequences,\"The X basis expressed in the Z basis: \n[[|±x⟩ = (|0⟩ ± |1⟩)/√2 &nbsp;⇒&nbsp; |⟨0|+x⟩|² = ½]]\nA measurement is a projection: after the result “+x” the state is |+x⟩ regardless of its past. That is why [[Z+ → X+ → Z]] gives ½ : ½ again. The operators S<sub>x</sub>, S<sub>z</sub> do not commute: \n[[[S<sub>x</sub>, S<sub>z</sub>] = −iħ S<sub>y</sub> ≠ 0]]\n\"\ntheory.2.predict,\"The state |+z⟩ measured along an axis n tilted by ϑ: \n[[P(+n) = |⟨+n|+z⟩|² = cos²(ϑ/2) = (1 + cos ϑ)/2]]\nThe half angle is the signature of spin ½: a 360° rotation multiplies the state by −1 (a global phase); only 720° brings it back exactly.\"\ntheory.3.core,\"Every pure qubit state: \n[[|ψ⟩ = cos(θ/2)|0⟩ + e<sup>iφ</sup> sin(θ/2)|1⟩, &nbsp; <i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i> = cos(θ/2), <i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i> = e<sup>iφ</sup> sin(θ/2)]]\nBloch vector: \n[[r = (sin θ cos φ, sin θ sin φ, cos θ) = (⟨X⟩, ⟨Y⟩, ⟨Z⟩)]]\nA gate = a rotation: \n[[R<sub>n</sub>(α) = e<sup>−iα n·σ/2</sup> = cos(α/2) I − i sin(α/2) (n·σ)]]\n\"\ntheory.3.puzzles,\"Pauli matrices: \n[[X = [0,1; 1,0], &nbsp; Y = [0,−i; i,0], &nbsp; Z = [1,0; 0,−1], &nbsp; H = (X + Z)/√2]]\nKey identities: \n[[X² = Y² = Z² = H² = I, &nbsp; HXH = Z, &nbsp; HZH = X, &nbsp; XY = iZ]]\nS = diag(1, i) = √Z, T = diag(1, e<sup>iπ/4</sup>) = √S. As rotations: X = −i·R<sub>x</sub>(π) ∼ R<sub>x</sub>(π).\"\ntheory.3.measure,\"Projective measurement in the Z basis: \n[[P(0) = |<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>|² = cos²(θ/2), &nbsp; P(1) = |<i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i>|² = sin²(θ/2), &nbsp; ⟨Z⟩ = P(0) − P(1) = cos θ]]\nFrom N measurements the estimate of P carries a statistical error \n[[σ ≈ √(P(1 − P)/N) &nbsp; (N = 100, P = ½ ⇒ σ = 5 %)]]\nφ can only be found by measuring [[⟨X⟩ = sin θ cos φ]] and [[⟨Y⟩ = sin θ sin φ]] — i.e. in other bases (tomography).\"\ntheory.3.lab,\"Measurement along an axis m: \n[[P(+m) = (1 + r·m)/2 = cos²(γ/2), &nbsp; γ = angle between r and m]]\nRotating the state (Rodrigues’ formula): \n[[r′ = r cos α + (n × r) sin α + n (n·r)(1 − cos α)]]\nAny gate decomposes into three rotations: U = e<sup>iγ</sup> R<sub>z</sub>(a) R<sub>y</sub>(b) R<sub>z</sub>(c).\"\ntheory.4.core,\"The density matrix and the Bloch vector: \n[[ρ = |ψ⟩⟨ψ| = [|<i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>|², <i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i><i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i>*; <i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>*<i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i>, |<i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i>|²] = ½(I + r·σ)]]\nDiagonal = populations, off-diagonal ρ₀₁ = (r<sub>x</sub> − i r<sub>y</sub>)/2 = coherence. Purity \n[[Tr ρ² = (1 + |r|²)/2 &nbsp; (1 = pure, ½ = maximally mixed)]]\n\"\ntheory.4.pure,\"\n[[|0⟩ →H→ (|0⟩ + |1⟩)/√2 →H→ ½(|0⟩ + |1⟩) + ½(|0⟩ − |1⟩) = |0⟩]]\nThe amplitudes towards |1⟩ (+½ and −½) cancel — two “paths” with opposite phase. In ρ after the first H: all four elements are ½ → the coherences [[ρ₀₁ = ½]] are maximal.\"\ntheory.4.withMeasure,\"A non-selective measurement (result forgotten) = projection onto the diagonal: \n[[ρ → Σ<sub>k</sub> P<sub>k</sub> ρ P<sub>k</sub> = [ρ₀₀, 0; 0, ρ₁₁]]]\nFor ρ = ½[1,1; 1,1] this gives I/2, and HIH† = I ⇒ P(0) = ½. A superposition and a mixture share the diagonal; they differ only off the diagonal.\"\ntheory.4.deco,\"The dephasing (phase-damping) channel plus a phase shift: \n[[ρ₀₁ → (1 − p) e<sup>−iφ</sup> ρ₀₁, &nbsp; ρ₀₀, ρ₁₁ unchanged]]\nAfter the second H: \n[[P(0) = ½ (1 + (1 − p) cos φ)]]\nThe fringe visibility V = [[(P<sub>max</sub> − P<sub>min</sub>)/(P<sub>max</sub> + P<sub>min</sub>) = 1 − p = 2|ρ₀₁|]]. In time: [[|ρ₀₁(t)| = |ρ₀₁(0)| e<sup>−t/T₂</sup>]].\"\ntheory.5.core,\"Dimension check like for matrices: ket = n×1, bra = 1×n, operator = n×n. \n[[⟨φ| = (|φ⟩)† = (φ₀*, φ₁*), &nbsp; ⟨φ|ψ⟩ = Σ<sub>k</sub> φ<sub>k</sub>* ψ<sub>k</sub> ∈ ℂ]]\nProperties: [[⟨φ|ψ⟩ = ⟨ψ|φ⟩*, ⟨ψ|ψ⟩ = 1]], completeness \n[[Σ<sub>k</sub> |k⟩⟨k| = I]]\n\"\ntheory.5.tasks,\"Spectral decomposition of an observable and the expectation value: \n[[A = Σ<sub>a</sub> a |a⟩⟨a|, &nbsp; ⟨A⟩ = ⟨ψ|A|ψ⟩ = Σ<sub>a</sub> a |⟨a|ψ⟩|² = Tr(ρA)]]\nThe projector [[P = |ψ⟩⟨ψ|]] satisfies [[P² = P, P† = P]]. Tensor product: \n[[(|a⟩ ⊗ |b⟩)<sub>ij</sub> = a<sub>i</sub> b<sub>j</sub>, &nbsp; dim(H<sub>A</sub> ⊗ H<sub>B</sub>) = dim H<sub>A</sub> · dim H<sub>B</sub>]]\nWave function: [[ψ(x) = ⟨x|ψ⟩]], ∫|ψ(x)|² dx = 1.\"\ntheory.6.core,\"A spin in the field B₀ (Zeeman levels, Larmor frequency): \n[[H = −γ B₀ S<sub>z</sub> = −(ħω₀/2) Z, &nbsp; ω₀ = γB₀, &nbsp; ΔE = ħω₀]]\nFor a proton [[γ/2π ≈ 42.58]] MHz/T ⇒ at [[1 T, ω₀/2π ≈ 42.6]] MHz. Time evolution: [[|ψ(t)⟩ = e<sup>−iHt/ħ</sup>|ψ(0)⟩]] = a rotation about z at angular speed ω₀.\"\ntheory.6.precession,\"\n[[<i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i>(t) = <i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i>(0) e<sup>iω₀t</sup> &nbsp;⇒&nbsp; |<i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i>(t)|² = const., &nbsp; φ(t) = φ(0) + ω₀t]]\nIn the 👁 “hands” view: <i class=\"\"cb\"\">β</i> turns relative to <i class=\"\"ca\"\">α</i>, their lengths stay the same. The rotating frame: [[|ψ̃⟩ = e<sup>−iω₀tZ/2</sup>|ψ⟩]] — in it the precession disappears.\"\ntheory.6.piPulse,\"An RF field B₁ cos(ωt) in the rotating frame (rotating-wave approximation): \n[[H̃ = (ħ/2)(Δ Z + Ω<sub>R</sub> X), &nbsp; Ω<sub>R</sub> = γB₁, &nbsp; Δ = ω₀ − ω]]\nAt resonance [[(Δ = 0)]]: \n[[P<sub>1</sub>(t) = sin²(Ω<sub>R</sub>t/2)]]\nA π pulse: [[Ω<sub>R</sub>t = π ⇒ |0⟩ → −i|1⟩ ∼ |1⟩]].\"\ntheory.6.halfPulse,\"\n[[R<sub>x</sub>(π/2)|0⟩ = (|0⟩ − i|1⟩)/√2 = |−i⟩]]\nA state on the equator: [[P(0) = P(1) = ½]], but [[|⟨X⟩|² + |⟨Y⟩|² = 1]] — maximal transverse magnetisation, hence the strongest NMR signal. That is why an NMR experiment starts with a π/2 pulse.\"\ntheory.6.tuning,\"Off resonance (Rabi’s formula): \n[[P<sub>1</sub>(t) = Ω<sub>R</sub>²/(Ω<sub>R</sub>² + Δ²) · sin²(√(Ω<sub>R</sub>² + Δ²) · t/2)]]\nThe rotation axis is tilted by [[tan ϑ = Δ/Ω<sub>R</sub>]], so the arrow never reaches the south pole. The resonance width ≈ Ω<sub>R</sub> — a weaker pulse means a sharper resonance.\"\ntheory.6.t2,\"The Bloch equations (1946): \n[[dM<sub>⊥</sub>/dt = … − M<sub>⊥</sub>/T₂, &nbsp; dM<sub>z</sub>/dt = … − (M<sub>z</sub> − M₀)/T₁]]\nThe FID signal: [[S(t) ∝ M<sub>⊥</sub>(0) cos(ω₀t) e<sup>−t/T₂</sup>]]. Always [[T₂ ≤ 2T₁]]. Spin echo (Hahn 1950): a π pulse at time τ reverses the dephasing caused by field inhomogeneity.\"\ntheory.7.core,\"Two qubits live in ℂ² ⊗ ℂ² (4 amplitudes). A product state: (a|0⟩ + b|1⟩) ⊗ (c|0⟩ + d|1⟩); an entangled state cannot be written that way. The criterion for \n[[|Ψ⟩ = Σ ψ<sub>ij</sub>|ij⟩: &nbsp; product ⇔ ψ₀₀ψ₁₁ − ψ₀₁ψ₁₀ = 0]]\nThe reduced state: [[ρ<sub>A</sub> = Tr<sub>B</sub> |Ψ⟩⟨Ψ|]].\"\ntheory.7.build,\"\n[[|00⟩ →H⊗I→ (|00⟩ + |10⟩)/√2 →CNOT→ (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2 = |Φ⁺⟩]]\nFor |Φ⁺⟩: [[ψ₀₀ψ₁₁ − ψ₀₁ψ₁₀ = ½ ≠ 0]] ⇒ entangled. \n[[ρ<sub>A</sub> = ρ<sub>B</sub> = I/2, &nbsp; S(ρ<sub>A</sub>) = −Tr ρ<sub>A</sub> log₂ ρ<sub>A</sub> = 1 bit]]\nAn entanglement entropy of 1 bit is the maximum for two qubits.\"\ntheory.7.noSignal,\"The correlation for |Φ⁺⟩ with axes a, b in the xz plane: \n[[E(a, b) = ⟨σ<sub>a</sub> ⊗ σ<sub>b</sub>⟩ = cos(a − b), &nbsp; P(same) = cos²((a − b)/2)]]\nNo-signalling: Bob’s statistics depend only on ρ<sub>B</sub>, which Alice’s local measurement does not change: \n[[Σ<sub>k</sub> Tr<sub>A</sub>[(P<sub>k</sub> ⊗ I) ρ] = ρ<sub>B</sub> = I/2]]\n\"\ntheory.7.chsh,\"The CHSH expression: \n[[S = E(a₀,b₀) + E(a₀,b₁) + E(a₁,b₀) − E(a₁,b₁)]]\nLocal hidden variables: [[|S| ≤ 2]]. Quantum (Tsirelson’s bound, 1980): [[|S| ≤ 2√2 ≈ 2.83]]. Winning the game: \n[[P<sub>win</sub> = ½ + S/8 &nbsp;⇒&nbsp; classical ≤ 75 %, quantum ≤ ½ + √2/4 = cos²(π/8) ≈ 85.4 %]]\nOptimum: [[a₀ = 0, a₁ = π/2, b₀ = π/4, b₁ = −π/4]].\"\ntheory.8.core,\"The postulates in short: (1) state = a unit vector |ψ⟩ (or ρ), (2) evolution: \n[[iħ ∂|ψ⟩/∂t = H|ψ⟩]]\n(3) measuring an observable A = Σ a P<sub>a</sub>: P(a) = ⟨ψ|P<sub>a</sub>|ψ⟩, the state afterwards P<sub>a</sub>|ψ⟩/√P(a) (“collapse”), (4) composite systems: the tensor product.\"\ntheory.8.statues,\"Robertson’s uncertainty relation: \n[[σ<sub>A</sub> σ<sub>B</sub> ≥ ½ |⟨[A, B]⟩|, &nbsp; [x, p] = iħ ⇒ σ<sub>x</sub> σ<sub>p</sub> ≥ ħ/2]]\nNoether’s theorem: if [[[H, G] = 0]] then d⟨G⟩/dt = 0 (a symmetry generated by [[G ⇒ G]] is conserved). Bohm’s guidance equation: dx/dt = (ħ/m) Im(∇ψ/ψ).\"\ntheory.8.sorting,\"Unitary evolution is reversible and linear; “collapse” is not unitary. Decoherence explains why interference between macro-alternatives is not seen (ρ becomes diagonal in the “pointer” basis), but it does not pick a single outcome — that is where interpretations (Copenhagen, Everett, Bohm, QBism…) part ways, while their predictions agree.\"\ntraps.hard.1.0.q,\"Two paths with amplitudes ½ and ½·e<sup>iπ/3</sup>. What is [[P = |A₁ + A₂|²]]?\"\ntraps.hard.1.0.options.0,\"¾\"\ntraps.hard.1.0.options.1,\"½\"\ntraps.hard.1.0.options.2,\"1\"\ntraps.hard.1.0.options.3,\"¼\"\ntraps.hard.1.0.why,\"[[P = cos²(Δφ/2) = cos²(π/6) = ¾]]. The interference term [[2 Re(A₁A₂*) = ½ cos(π/3) = ¼]].\"\ntraps.hard.1.1.q,\"What is i<sup>i</sup> (principal value)?\"\ntraps.hard.1.1.options.0,\"[[e<sup>−π/2</sup> ≈ 0.208]] — a real number\"\ntraps.hard.1.1.options.1,\"i\"\ntraps.hard.1.1.options.2,\"−1\"\ntraps.hard.1.1.options.3,\"undefined\"\ntraps.hard.1.1.why,\"[[i = e<sup>iπ/2</sup> ⇒ i<sup>i</sup> = e<sup>i·iπ/2</sup> = e<sup>−π/2</sup>]]. Euler found this in 1746.\"\ntraps.hard.2.0.q,\"The state |+z⟩ is measured along x. What are ⟨S<sub>x</sub>⟩ and the standard deviation σ(S<sub>x</sub>)?\"\ntraps.hard.2.0.options.0,\"[[⟨S<sub>x</sub>⟩ = 0, σ = ħ/2]]\"\ntraps.hard.2.0.options.1,\"[[⟨S<sub>x</sub>⟩ = ħ/2, σ = 0]]\"\ntraps.hard.2.0.options.2,\"[[⟨S<sub>x</sub>⟩ = 0, σ = 0]]\"\ntraps.hard.2.0.why,\"Outcomes ±ħ/2 at 50 % each ⇒ mean 0, deviation ħ/2. Along z it would be [[σ(S<sub>z</sub>) = 0]].\"\ntraps.hard.2.1.q,\"Why does spin ½ involve the half angle: [[P = cos²(ϑ/2)]]?\"\ntraps.hard.2.1.options.0,\"orthogonal states (|+z⟩, |−z⟩) are antipodal on the Bloch sphere, so 180° = “90° in Hilbert space”\"\ntraps.hard.2.1.options.1,\"because of a magnet defect\"\ntraps.hard.2.1.options.2,\"because the spin rotates at half speed\"\ntraps.hard.2.1.why,\"The angle in Hilbert space is half the angle on the sphere: [[|⟨+n|+z⟩| = cos(ϑ/2)]].\"\ntraps.hard.3.0.q,\"What is HZH?\"\ntraps.hard.3.0.options.0,\"X\"\ntraps.hard.3.0.options.1,\"Z\"\ntraps.hard.3.0.options.2,\"Y\"\ntraps.hard.3.0.options.3,\"I\"\ntraps.hard.3.0.why,\"H swaps the axes [[x ↔ z]], so a rotation about z becomes a rotation about x. Hence [[H·Z·H = X]] (puzzle 5).\"\ntraps.hard.3.1.q,\"A state with [[θ = π/3, φ = 0]]. What is P(0) in the Z basis?\"\ntraps.hard.3.1.options.0,\"[[cos²(π/6) = ¾]]\"\ntraps.hard.3.1.options.1,\"[[cos²(π/3) = ¼]]\"\ntraps.hard.3.1.options.2,\"½\"\ntraps.hard.3.1.options.3,\"[[sin²(π/6) = ¼]]\"\ntraps.hard.3.1.why,\"[[P(0) = cos²(θ/2) = (1 + cos θ)/2 = (1 + ½)/2 = ¾]].\"\ntraps.hard.4.0.q,\"What is the purity Tr ρ² of the maximally mixed qubit state I/2?\"\ntraps.hard.4.0.options.0,\"½\"\ntraps.hard.4.0.options.1,\"1\"\ntraps.hard.4.0.options.2,\"0\"\ntraps.hard.4.0.options.3,\"¼\"\ntraps.hard.4.0.why,\"[[Tr (I/2)² = Tr I/4 = ½]]. In general [[Tr ρ² = (1 + |r|²)/2]], and [[r = 0]] at the centre.\"\ntraps.hard.4.1.q,\"Dephasing [[p = 0.4]] and phase shift [[φ = 0]]. What is P(0) at the end of H → (environment) → H?\"\ntraps.hard.4.1.options.0,\"0.8\"\ntraps.hard.4.1.options.1,\"0.6\"\ntraps.hard.4.1.options.2,\"0.7\"\ntraps.hard.4.1.options.3,\"1\"\ntraps.hard.4.1.why,\"[[P(0) = ½(1 + (1 − p) cos φ) = ½(1 + 0.6) = 0.8]].\"\ntraps.hard.5.0.q,\"What is the expectation value ⟨+|Z|+⟩?\"\ntraps.hard.5.0.options.0,\"0\"\ntraps.hard.5.0.options.1,\"1\"\ntraps.hard.5.0.options.2,\"−1\"\ntraps.hard.5.0.options.3,\"½\"\ntraps.hard.5.0.why,\"[[⟨Z⟩ = P(0)·(+1) + P(1)·(−1) = ½ − ½ = 0]] — although single measurements give only ±1.\"\ntraps.hard.5.1.q,\"How many complex amplitudes does a composite state of three qubits have?\"\ntraps.hard.5.1.options.0,\"2³ = 8\"\ntraps.hard.5.1.options.1,\"3 × 2 = 6\"\ntraps.hard.5.1.options.2,\"3\"\ntraps.hard.5.1.options.3,\"2\"\ntraps.hard.5.1.why,\"Dimensions multiply under ⊗: 2 · 2 · 2 = 8 (exponential growth — that is why classical simulation is hard).\"\ntraps.hard.6.0.q,\"Rabi frequency Ω<sub>R</sub>, detuning [[Δ = Ω<sub>R</sub>]]. What is the maximum of P(|1⟩)?\"\ntraps.hard.6.0.options.0,\"½\"\ntraps.hard.6.0.options.1,\"1\"\ntraps.hard.6.0.options.2,\"¼\"\ntraps.hard.6.0.options.3,\"0\"\ntraps.hard.6.0.why,\"[[P<sub>max</sub> = Ω<sub>R</sub>²/(Ω<sub>R</sub>² + Δ²) = 1/2]].\"\ntraps.hard.6.1.q,\"How long does it take for the transverse magnetisation to fall to 1/e?\"\ntraps.hard.6.1.options.0,\"T₂\"\ntraps.hard.6.1.options.1,\"T₁\"\ntraps.hard.6.1.options.2,\"2π/ω₀\"\ntraps.hard.6.1.options.3,\"π/Ω<sub>R</sub>\"\ntraps.hard.6.1.why,\"[[M<sub>⊥</sub>(t) = M<sub>⊥</sub>(0) e<sup>−t/T₂</sup>]]. T₁ governs the return of M<sub>z</sub>; always [[T₂ ≤ 2T₁]].\"\ntraps.hard.7.0.q,\"What is the quantum (Tsirelson) bound of the CHSH expression S?\"\ntraps.hard.7.0.options.0,\"2√2 ≈ 2.83\"\ntraps.hard.7.0.options.1,\"2\"\ntraps.hard.7.0.options.2,\"4\"\ntraps.hard.7.0.options.3,\"√2\"\ntraps.hard.7.0.why,\"Locally [[|S| ≤ 2]], quantum [[|S| ≤ 2√2]], algebraically up to 4 (PR boxes) — nature stops at 2√2.\"\ntraps.hard.7.1.q,\"Is the state ½(|00⟩ + |01⟩ + |10⟩ + |11⟩) entangled?\"\ntraps.hard.7.1.options.0,\"no — it is |+⟩ ⊗ |+⟩\"\ntraps.hard.7.1.options.1,\"yes, because it has four terms\"\ntraps.hard.7.1.options.2,\"yes, maximally\"\ntraps.hard.7.1.why,\"[[ψ₀₀ψ₁₁ − ψ₀₁ψ₁₀ = ¼ − ¼ = 0]] ⇒ a product. The number of terms does not decide entanglement.\"\ntraps.hard.8.0.q,\"What follows from [[[x, p] = iħ]]?\"\ntraps.hard.8.0.options.0,\"[[σ<sub>x</sub> σ<sub>p</sub> ≥ ħ/2]] for every state\"\ntraps.hard.8.0.options.1,\"every position measurement disturbs momentum by exactly ħ\"\ntraps.hard.8.0.options.2,\"x and p cannot be measured at all\"\ntraps.hard.8.0.why,\"Robertson: [[σ<sub>A</sub>σ<sub>B</sub> ≥ ½|⟨[A,B]⟩| = ħ/2]]. It is a property of the state (the spread of outcomes), not of instrument disturbance.\"\ntraps.hard.8.1.q,\"What does decoherence NOT explain?\"\ntraps.hard.8.1.options.0,\"why we get this particular single outcome\"\ntraps.hard.8.1.options.1,\"why macroscopic superpositions do not interfere\"\ntraps.hard.8.1.options.2,\"why the off-diagonal of ρ vanishes\"\ntraps.hard.8.1.why,\"Decoherence diagonalises ρ but does not pick a single outcome (the measurement problem) — that is where interpretations diverge.\"\nscrolls.s1a.title,\"The square root of a negative number\"\nscrolls.s1a.text,\"In <i>Ars Magna</i> (1545), solving “divide 10 into two parts whose product is 40”, Cardano obtained 5 ± √−15. The result worked, but he did not know what it meant. Bombelli, in <i>L’Algebra</i> (1572), was the first to give rules for calculating with “più di meno” (today’s +i) and “meno di meno” (−i) — and showed that they lead to perfectly real roots of cubic equations.\"\nscrolls.s1a.quote,\"“So progresses arithmetic subtlety, the end of which, as is said, is as refined as it is useless.” — Cardano on √−15\"\nscrolls.s1b.title,\"“Imaginary” and Euler’s formula\"\nscrolls.s1b.text,\"In <i>La Géométrie</i> (1637) Descartes called these roots, dismissively, “imaginary” — and the name stuck. In <i>Introductio in analysin infinitorum</i> (1748) Euler published [[e<sup>ix</sup> = cos x + i sin x]], and in 1777 he introduced the letter <b>i</b> for √−1. Already in a letter to Goldbach (1746) he had computed that i<sup>i</sup> is a real number ≈ 0.2079.\"\nscrolls.s1b.quote,\"“Our jewel … the most remarkable formula in mathematics.” — Richard Feynman on [[e<sup>iθ</sup> = cos θ + i sin θ]] (The Feynman Lectures, 1963)\"\nscrolls.s1c.title,\"Numbers as arrows, probability as a square\"\nscrolls.s1c.text,\"The surveyor Wessel (1799) and the bookkeeper Argand (1806) independently drew complex numbers as points of a plane — multiplication became rotation. Gauss (1831) gave them the name “complex”. In 1926 Max Born added in a footnote of a paper on collisions that the probability is proportional to the <b>square</b> of the amplitude — today’s Born rule (Nobel Prize 1954).\"\nscrolls.s1c.quote,\"“The motion of particles follows probability laws but the probability itself propagates according to the law of causality.” — Max Born, 1926\"\nscrolls.s2a.title,\"A postcard to Bohr\"\nscrolls.s2a.text,\"Stern proposed the experiment in 1921, aiming to disprove Bohr’s “space quantisation”. After months of failure, Gerlach in Frankfurt saw, in February 1922, the deposit on the glass plate split in two. He sent Bohr a postcard with a micrograph of the two spots. (Only later did it turn out that the effect was not orbital angular momentum but electron spin.)\"\nscrolls.s2a.quote,\"“Attached is the experimental proof of directional quantisation. We congratulate you on the confirmation of your theory.” — Gerlach to Bohr, 8 Feb 1922\"\nscrolls.s2b.title,\"A cheap cigar\"\nscrolls.s2b.text,\"The silver layer on the plate was so thin that it could not be seen. According to Stern’s recollection, the trace appeared only when he leaned over the plate with a cheap cigar: sulphur from the smoke turned the silver into black silver sulphide. The physicists Friedrich and Herschbach repeated the cigar trick in 2003 — it works (an anecdote, but a tested one).\"\nscrolls.s2b.quote,\"On an assistant’s salary Stern smoked cheap cigars with a high sulphur content — and that is what saved the experiment. — after Friedrich and Herschbach, Physics Today (2003)\"\nscrolls.s2c.title,\"Spin: a foolishness the young can afford\"\nscrolls.s2c.text,\"In 1925 Uhlenbeck and Goudsmit proposed that the electron has an intrinsic angular momentum ±ħ/2. When Lorentz calculated that the electron’s “surface” would spin faster than light, they wanted to withdraw the paper — Ehrenfest had already sent it. Pauli was against it at first (he had talked Kronig out of the same idea). Phipps and Taylor (1927) repeated the Stern–Gerlach experiment with hydrogen atoms and confirmed that the two spots come from electron spin.\"\nscrolls.s2c.quote,\"“You are both young enough to be able to afford a stupidity.” — Ehrenfest to Uhlenbeck and Goudsmit, 1925\"\nscrolls.s3a.title,\"A sphere before the qubit\"\nscrolls.s3a.text,\"Optics knew the same sphere: Poincaré (1892) mapped every polarisation of light to a point of a sphere — circular polarisation at the poles, linear on the equator. In 1927 Pauli wrote spin using three matrices σ<sub>x</sub>, σ<sub>y</sub>, σ<sub>z</sub>; their linear combinations are exactly the rotations of the Bloch sphere.\"\nscrolls.s3a.quote,\"“Mathematics is the art of giving the same name to different things.” — Henri Poincaré\"\nscrolls.s3b.title,\"Nuclear induction and a geometric picture\"\nscrolls.s3b.text,\"In 1946 Bloch (Stanford) and Purcell (Harvard) independently discovered nuclear magnetic resonance in matter; they shared the Nobel Prize in 1952. Bloch described the motion of magnetisation as the rotation of a vector. Feynman, Vernon and Hellwarth (1957) showed that <i>any</i> two-level system can be drawn as an arrow in a ball — which is why we speak of the Bloch sphere of a qubit today.\"\nscrolls.s3b.quote,\"Heisenberg: “Space is the field of linear operations.” Bloch: “Nonsense. Space is blue and birds fly through it.” — Bloch recalling a conversation with Heisenberg (Physics Today, 1976)\"\nscrolls.s3c.title,\"The word “qubit”\"\nscrolls.s3c.text,\"The word <b>qubit</b> was introduced by Benjamin Schumacher in the paper <i>Quantum coding</i> (1995) — reportedly as a joke from a conversation with William Wootters (it sounds like the biblical unit “cubit”). In the same paper he showed that the qubit is the unit of quantum information, just as the bit is the unit of classical information.\"\nscrolls.s3c.quote,\"“The term ‘qubit’ was coined in jest during one of the author’s many intriguing and valuable conversations with W. K. Wootters.” — Schumacher, Quantum coding (1995)\"\nscrolls.s4a.title,\"Two slits and feeble light\"\nscrolls.s4a.text,\"Young (lectures 1801–1803 at the Royal Society) showed fringes behind two slits and thus the wave nature of light — against Newton’s authority. Taylor (1909) repeated the interference with light so feeble that on average less than one photon was inside the apparatus at a time; the exposure took three months and the fringes remained.\"\nscrolls.s4a.quote,\"“The experiments I am about to relate … may be repeated with great ease, whenever the sun shines.” — Thomas Young, 1803\"\nscrolls.s4b.title,\"Electrons one at a time\"\nscrolls.s4b.text,\"Jönsson (1961) was first to perform the double-slit experiment with electrons. Merli, Missiroli and Pozzi (Bologna, 1974) and Tonomura (Hitachi, 1989) sent electrons <i>one at a time</i>: each lands as a dot, yet thousands of dots build up fringes. In 2002 the readers of Physics World voted it the most beautiful experiment in physics.\"\nscrolls.s4b.quote,\"“A phenomenon which is impossible, absolutely impossible, to explain in any classical way, and which has in it the heart of quantum mechanics. In reality, it contains the only mystery.” — Feynman, 1965\"\nscrolls.s4c.title,\"The density matrix and decoherence\"\nscrolls.s4c.text,\"The density matrix was introduced independently by von Neumann and Landau (1927) to describe mixtures and subsystems. Zeh (1970) noticed that macroscopic systems cannot be isolated from their environment, and Zurek (1981–82) showed how the environment selects a “pointer” basis and wipes out coherences. Haroche and colleagues measured decoherence “live” in the lab (1996).\"\nscrolls.s4c.quote,\"“Quantum theory is the only one we have; decoherence shows why the world looks classical to us.” — a paraphrase of W. Zurek’s idea\"\nscrolls.s5a.title,\"A Poisson bracket on a walk\"\nscrolls.s5a.text,\"In the summer of 1925 the 23-year-old Dirac received the proofs of Heisenberg’s paper. On a Sunday walk he realised that the commutator xp − px corresponds to the classical Poisson bracket — but the library was closed on Sunday, so he had to wait until Monday to check the formula. In 1930 he published <i>The Principles of Quantum Mechanics</i>; he added the bra-ket notation in 1939.\"\nscrolls.s5a.quote,\"“It is more important to have beauty in one’s equations than to have them fit experiment.” — Dirac, Scientific American, 1963\"\nscrolls.s5b.title,\"The equation that predicted antimatter\"\nscrolls.s5b.text,\"Dirac’s relativistic equation (1928) had solutions with negative energy. Eventually (1931) Dirac saw a new particle in them — the anti-electron. Anderson found it in cosmic rays in 1932 (the positron). Dirac shared the 1933 Nobel Prize with Schrödinger; he considered declining it because of the publicity, until Rutherford told him that declining would attract even more.\"\nscrolls.s5b.quote,\"“That was not a question, it was a comment.” — anecdote: Dirac, after someone in a lecture said they did not understand the equation in the top right\"\nscrolls.s6a.title,\"Lines that split\"\nscrolls.s6a.text,\"Zeeman (1896) placed a sodium flame between the poles of an electromagnet and the spectral lines broadened, later split. Lorentz explained it at once by the motion of charged particles in the atom and estimated their charge-to-mass ratio from the splitting — at about the time Thomson discovered the electron (1897). Nobel Prize 1902. The so-called “anomalous” Zeeman effect was explained only by spin.\"\nscrolls.s6a.quote,\"“How can one look happy when one is thinking about the anomalous Zeeman effect?” — Pauli, around 1923\"\nscrolls.s6b.title,\"Atoms listen to the radio\"\nscrolls.s6b.text,\"At Columbia University (1938) Rabi’s group sent a molecular beam through a static field and an oscillating radio-frequency field. At exactly the right frequency the spins flipped and the beam missed the detector — the first measurement of magnetic resonance. Nobel Prize 1944. Atomic clocks, NMR and MRI all grew out of the method.\"\nscrolls.s6b.quote,\"“Who ordered that?” — Rabi on the discovery of the muon (1936), a particle nobody expected\"\nscrolls.s6c.title,\"An echo, an image of the body, and Shor in a test tube\"\nscrolls.s6c.text,\"Hahn (1950) discovered the spin echo — a π pulse reverses the dephasing and the lost signal comes back. Lauterbur (1973) and Mansfield turned NMR into imaging (MRI; Nobel Prize 2003). In 2001 a team at IBM and Stanford (Vandersypen, Chuang et al.) ran Shor’s algorithm in an NMR molecule with 7 nuclear spins and factored 15 = 3 × 5.\"\nscrolls.s6c.quote,\"“The echo was an accident — pulses I applied for other reasons produced a signal that should not have been there.” — a paraphrase of Hahn’s recollection\"\nscrolls.s7a.title,\"God does not play dice?\"\nscrolls.s7a.text,\"In a letter to Max Born (1926) Einstein rejected the idea that nature is fundamentally random. In May 1935 the EPR paper appeared: “Can Quantum-Mechanical Description of Physical Reality Be Considered Complete?”. Bohr replied in October 1935 with a paper of the <i>same</i> title: one cannot speak of reality without the experimental arrangement.\"\nscrolls.s7a.quote,\"“The theory says a lot, but does not really bring us any closer to the secret of the Old One. I, at any rate, am convinced that He does not throw dice.” — Einstein to Born, 4 Dec 1926\"\nscrolls.s7b.title,\"Verschränkung and spooks\"\nscrolls.s7b.text,\"In the summer of 1935 Schrödinger and Einstein exchanged letters about EPR. Schrödinger coined the word <i>Verschränkung</i> — entanglement — for the phenomenon, and in the same year also the cat in the box. As late as 1947 Einstein wrote to Born about “spooky action at a distance” (<i>spukhafte Fernwirkung</i>).\"\nscrolls.s7b.quote,\"“I would not call that one but rather <i>the</i> characteristic trait of quantum mechanics, the one that enforces its entire departure from classical lines of thought.” — Schrödinger on entanglement, 1935\"\nscrolls.s7c.title,\"From an inequality to a Nobel Prize\"\nscrolls.s7c.text,\"Bell (1964, during a sabbatical in the USA) derived an inequality that every local hidden-variable theory must satisfy. CHSH (1969) adapted it for real instruments; Freedman and Clauser (1972) violated it for the first time. Aspect (1982) switched the analysers while the photons were in flight. In 2015 three teams (Delft, Vienna, NIST) closed the main “loopholes” simultaneously. Nobel Prize 2022: Aspect, Clauser, Zeilinger.\"\nscrolls.s7c.quote,\"“But in 1952 I saw the impossible done.” — Bell on Bohm’s theory, which refuted von Neumann’s “proof” against hidden variables\"\nscrolls.s8a.title,\"Como and Solvay\"\nscrolls.s8a.text,\"In September 1927 at Como, Bohr presented complementarity for the first time; Heisenberg published the uncertainty principle the same year. A month later, at the 5th Solvay Conference in Brussels, Einstein proposed every morning at breakfast a thought experiment meant to beat the uncertainty principle — and by evening Bohr had found the flaw. At the 6th conference (1930) Bohr refuted Einstein’s “photon box” using Einstein’s own general relativity.\"\nscrolls.s8a.quote,\"“Einstein, stop telling God what to do!” — Bohr (as recalled by witnesses of the Solvay Conference, 1927)\"\nscrolls.s8b.title,\"The thinkers in the hall\"\nscrolls.s8b.text,\"Kant: <i>Critique of Pure Reason</i> (1781). Noether: the theorem on symmetries and conservation laws, <i>Invariante Variationsprobleme</i> (1918) — in his 1935 obituary Einstein called her the most significant creative mathematical genius since the higher education of women began. Wittgenstein: <i>Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus</i> (1921), proposition 5.6 on the limits of language. Heisenberg: <i>Physics and Philosophy</i> (1958), the Gifford Lectures.\"\nscrolls.s8b.quote,\"“What we observe is not nature itself, but nature exposed to our method of questioning.” — Heisenberg, 1958\"\nscrolls.s8c.title,\"Interpretations multiply\"\nscrolls.s8c.text,\"Von Neumann (1932) put “collapse” (projection) into the formalism, together with an alleged proof that hidden variables are impossible. Bohm (1952) got around it with a non-local pilot wave. Everett (1957, PhD thesis) proposed that no collapse happens at all (later the “many-worlds” interpretation). Mermin (1989) summed up many physicists’ attitude with a phrase often misattributed to Feynman.\"\nscrolls.s8c.quote,\"“If I were forced to sum up in one sentence what the Copenhagen interpretation says to me, it would be ‘Shut up and calculate!’” — N. David Mermin, Physics Today, 1989\"\ntraps.ancient.1.q,\"Who first gave rules for calculating with square roots of negative numbers?\"\ntraps.ancient.1.options.0,\"Rafael Bombelli (1572)\"\ntraps.ancient.1.options.1,\"Isaac Newton (1687)\"\ntraps.ancient.1.options.2,\"Carl F. Gauss (1831)\"\ntraps.ancient.1.why,\"Bombelli in L’Algebra (1572); Gauss later popularised the name “complex numbers”.\"\ntraps.ancient.2.q,\"According to Stern’s recollection, what made the silver trace visible?\"\ntraps.ancient.2.options.0,\"sulphur from a cheap cigar\"\ntraps.ancient.2.options.1,\"an ultraviolet lamp\"\ntraps.ancient.2.options.2,\"a photographic emulsion\"\ntraps.ancient.2.why,\"Silver sulphide is black — the layer became visible (Friedrich and Herschbach repeated it in 2003).\"\ntraps.ancient.3.q,\"Who shared the 1952 Nobel Prize with Bloch for nuclear magnetic resonance?\"\ntraps.ancient.3.options.0,\"Edward Purcell\"\ntraps.ancient.3.options.1,\"Richard Feynman\"\ntraps.ancient.3.options.2,\"Max Born\"\ntraps.ancient.3.why,\"Bloch (Stanford) and Purcell (Harvard) independently discovered NMR in matter in 1946.\"\ntraps.ancient.4.q,\"Who first demonstrated the interference of light behind two slits?\"\ntraps.ancient.4.options.0,\"Thomas Young (1801–1803)\"\ntraps.ancient.4.options.1,\"Isaac Newton (1704)\"\ntraps.ancient.4.options.2,\"Richard Feynman (1965)\"\ntraps.ancient.4.why,\"Young — thereby supporting the wave theory of light against Newton’s corpuscular one.\"\ntraps.ancient.5.q,\"Which particle did the Dirac equation predict, and who found it?\"\ntraps.ancient.5.options.0,\"the positron — Carl Anderson (1932)\"\ntraps.ancient.5.options.1,\"the neutron — James Chadwick (1932)\"\ntraps.ancient.5.options.2,\"the muon — Rabi’s team (1936)\"\ntraps.ancient.5.why,\"Negative-energy solutions → the anti-electron, found in cosmic rays in 1932.\"\ntraps.ancient.6.q,\"Who ran Shor’s algorithm in NMR in 2001, and what did they factor?\"\ntraps.ancient.6.options.0,\"an IBM–Stanford team — 15 = 3 × 5\"\ntraps.ancient.6.options.1,\"Rabi — 21 = 3 × 7\"\ntraps.ancient.6.options.2,\"Bloch and Purcell — 6 = 2 × 3\"\ntraps.ancient.6.why,\"Vandersypen, Chuang et al.: a molecule with 7 nuclear spins, Nature 2001.\"\ntraps.ancient.7.q,\"Who coined the word “entanglement” (Verschränkung), and when?\"\ntraps.ancient.7.options.0,\"Erwin Schrödinger, 1935\"\ntraps.ancient.7.options.1,\"Albert Einstein, 1926\"\ntraps.ancient.7.options.2,\"John Bell, 1964\"\ntraps.ancient.7.why,\"In his correspondence with Einstein and in his 1935 papers, after the EPR paper.\"\ntraps.ancient.8.q,\"Where and when did Bohr first present complementarity?\"\ntraps.ancient.8.options.0,\"Como, September 1927\"\ntraps.ancient.8.options.1,\"Copenhagen, 1913\"\ntraps.ancient.8.options.2,\"Brussels, 1930\"\ntraps.ancient.8.why,\"A lecture at the Volta centenary conference in Como; the Solvay Conference followed a month later.\"\ntraps.1.0.q,\"Amplitude [[α = 0.6i]]. What is the probability of the corresponding outcome?\"\ntraps.1.0.options.0,\"0.36\"\ntraps.1.0.options.1,\"−0.36\"\ntraps.1.0.options.2,\"0.6\"\ntraps.1.0.why,\"[[|α|² = α·α* = 0.6i · (−0.6i) = 0.36]]. A probability is never negative.\"\ntraps.1.1.q,\"What does multiplying an amplitude by the factor e<sup>iφ</sup> mean?\"\ntraps.1.1.options.0,\"rotating the “hand” in the complex plane by the angle φ\"\ntraps.1.1.options.1,\"increasing the probability\"\ntraps.1.1.options.2,\"physically spinning the particle\"\ntraps.1.1.why,\"The magnitude (and hence |α|²) does not change, only the phase does.\"\ntraps.1.2.q,\"Which sentence is correct?\"\ntraps.1.2.options.0,\"Amplitudes interfere; only afterwards do we compute the probability.\"\ntraps.1.2.options.1,\"Probabilities interfere.\"\ntraps.1.2.options.2,\"Interference is a purely quantum phenomenon; classical waves don’t have it.\"\ntraps.1.2.why,\"Classical waves interfere too — but in quantum mechanics it is probability amplitudes that interfere.\"\ntraps.2.0.q,\"The Stern–Gerlach experiment showed…\"\ntraps.2.0.options.0,\"two separate spots when measuring along a chosen direction\"\ntraps.2.0.options.1,\"how the electron really spins\"\ntraps.2.0.options.2,\"a continuous band of all deflections\"\ntraps.2.0.why,\"It showed the outcomes of a precisely specified measurement, not a “movie” of a spinning electron.\"\ntraps.2.1.q,\"An atom passes a “+z” filter, then we measure along x. What holds?\"\ntraps.2.1.options.0,\"+x and −x 50 % each\"\ntraps.2.1.options.1,\"always +x\"\ntraps.2.1.options.2,\"always +z\"\ntraps.2.1.why,\"Certainty in one basis does not mean certainty in another.\"\ntraps.2.2.q,\"What does “spin ½” mean?\"\ntraps.2.2.options.0,\"a kind of quantum spin with two possible projections\"\ntraps.2.2.options.1,\"half a turn of the electron\"\ntraps.2.2.options.2,\"half the rotation speed\"\ntraps.2.2.why,\"It names the type of spin, not a mechanical rotation.\"\ntraps.3.0.q,\"What is the correct way to say what the Hadamard gate does?\"\ntraps.3.0.options.0,\"it performs a unitary rotation that swaps the x and z axes\"\ntraps.3.0.options.1,\"it always creates a superposition\"\ntraps.3.0.options.2,\"it measures the qubit\"\ntraps.3.0.why,\"[[H|+⟩ = |0⟩]] — so it also “undoes” a superposition. It depends on the basis.\"\ntraps.3.1.q,\"−iX ∼ X means…\"\ntraps.3.1.options.0,\"equal up to a global phase (physically identical)\"\ntraps.3.1.options.1,\"I can turn one into the other\"\ntraps.3.1.options.2,\"they differ by a relative phase\"\ntraps.3.1.why,\"The factor −i is common to the whole state → a global phase, unobservable.\"\ntraps.3.2.q,\"Where do the orthogonal states |0⟩ and |1⟩ lie on the Bloch sphere?\"\ntraps.3.2.options.0,\"at opposite poles\"\ntraps.3.2.options.1,\"perpendicular to each other (90°)\"\ntraps.3.2.options.2,\"at the same point\"\ntraps.3.2.why,\"Perpendicular in Hilbert space, antipodal on the Bloch sphere (because of θ/2).\"\ntraps.3.3.q,\"The Z gate applied to |+⟩…\"\ntraps.3.3.options.0,\"turns it into |−⟩; P(0) and P(1) in the Z basis stay ½\"\ntraps.3.3.options.1,\"changes nothing, because the probabilities are the same\"\ntraps.3.3.options.2,\"flips |0⟩ to |1⟩\"\ntraps.3.3.why,\"A rotation about z changes the relative phase — you can tell by measuring in the X basis.\"\ntraps.4.0.q,\"We insert a Z-basis measurement between two H gates and forget the result. Why don’t we get 0 with certainty any more?\"\ntraps.4.0.options.0,\"the measurement destroyed the coherence (off-diagonal elements of ρ)\"\ntraps.4.0.options.1,\"the measurement broke the gate\"\ntraps.4.0.options.2,\"the qubit got tired\"\ntraps.4.0.why,\"Without a phase relation, the second H has nothing to turn into interference.\"\ntraps.4.1.q,\"The density matrix is denoted…\"\ntraps.4.1.options.0,\"ρ (rho)\"\ntraps.4.1.options.1,\"p\"\ntraps.4.1.options.2,\"P\"\ntraps.4.1.why,\"Greek rho. The letter p is momentum or probability.\"\ntraps.4.2.q,\"Which sentence is correct?\"\ntraps.4.2.options.0,\"A superposition and a mixture can have the same Z statistics but differ under interference.\"\ntraps.4.2.options.1,\"Superposition means we don’t know which state the qubit is in.\"\ntraps.4.2.options.2,\"A quantum computer tries all possibilities and picks the right one.\"\ntraps.4.2.why,\"Superposition is not ignorance, and quantum parallelism is not 2ⁿ processors.\"\ntraps.5.0.q,\"What is ⟨φ|ψ⟩?\"\ntraps.5.0.options.0,\"a complex number (amplitude)\"\ntraps.5.0.options.1,\"an operator\"\ntraps.5.0.options.2,\"a probability\"\ntraps.5.0.why,\"The probability is |⟨φ|ψ⟩|².\"\ntraps.5.1.q,\"What is |ψ⟩⟨φ|?\"\ntraps.5.1.options.0,\"an operator (matrix)\"\ntraps.5.1.options.1,\"a number\"\ntraps.5.1.options.2,\"a state\"\ntraps.5.1.why,\"Column times row = matrix. The order carries meaning!\"\ntraps.5.2.q,\"The wave function ψ(x) is…\"\ntraps.5.2.options.0,\"the representation of the state in the position basis: ⟨x|ψ⟩\"\ntraps.5.2.options.1,\"the abstract state itself\"\ntraps.5.2.options.2,\"the probability of finding the particle\"\ntraps.5.2.why,\"The same |ψ⟩ also has a momentum representation ⟨p|ψ⟩.\"\ntraps.6.0.q,\"What “oscillates” in Rabi oscillations?\"\ntraps.6.0.options.0,\"the amplitudes and probabilities of the outcomes\"\ntraps.6.0.options.1,\"the electron between two positions\"\ntraps.6.0.options.2,\"the magnet in the apparatus\"\ntraps.6.0.why,\"Nothing swings mechanically — the state changes.\"\ntraps.6.1.q,\"Precession about the z axis, when measuring in the Z basis…\"\ntraps.6.1.options.0,\"changes neither P(0) nor P(1); it changes the phase\"\ntraps.6.1.options.1,\"flips the spin\"\ntraps.6.1.options.2,\"increases the energy\"\ntraps.6.1.why,\"Rotation about z = change of the relative phase.\"\ntraps.6.2.q,\"The NMR signal (e.g. SpinQ) corresponds to…\"\ntraps.6.2.options.0,\"the expectation value of the transverse magnetisation of the ensemble\"\ntraps.6.2.options.1,\"a single outcome of a single molecule\"\ntraps.6.2.options.2,\"the collapse of one spin\"\ntraps.6.2.why,\"We measure a whole collection of molecules at once.\"\ntraps.6.3.q,\"Because of T₂ the Bloch vector shrinks to half its length. The state is…\"\ntraps.6.3.options.0,\"mixed (inside the sphere)\"\ntraps.6.3.options.1,\"still pure\"\ntraps.6.3.options.2,\"invalid\"\ntraps.6.3.why,\"|r| < 1 → mixed state. Normalisation (trace of [[ρ = 1]]) still holds.\"\ntraps.7.0.q,\"Alice changes her measurement axis. What does Bob see in his results?\"\ntraps.7.0.options.0,\"still 50/50 — nothing changes\"\ntraps.7.0.options.1,\"an instant change in the statistics\"\ntraps.7.0.options.2,\"a message from Alice\"\ntraps.7.0.why,\"Entanglement does not allow signalling. The correlations show only after comparing.\"\ntraps.7.1.q,\"Why does an entangled qubit have no arrow on the Bloch sphere?\"\ntraps.7.1.options.0,\"it has no pure state of its own — its reduced state is a mixture\"\ntraps.7.1.options.1,\"because it is broken\"\ntraps.7.1.options.2,\"because it spins too fast\"\ntraps.7.1.why,\"The whole is pure, the part is mixed — “the whole is more than the sum of its parts”.\"\ntraps.7.2.q,\"A violation of Bell’s inequality means…\"\ntraps.7.2.options.0,\"one cannot keep both locality and pre-existing values\"\ntraps.7.2.options.1,\"quantum mechanics is refuted\"\ntraps.7.2.options.2,\"information travels faster than light\"\ntraps.7.2.why,\"Bohm chooses non-locality; the operational approach abandons pre-existing values.\"\ntraps.8.0.q,\"“The electron doesn’t exist until we look at it.” Is this an accurate statement of Bohr’s view?\"\ntraps.8.0.options.0,\"No — Bohr demanded stating the experimental conditions under which a phenomenon is described.\"\ntraps.8.0.options.1,\"Yes, that is exactly what he claimed.\"\ntraps.8.0.why,\"Bohr cared about unambiguous communication of results, not about denying existence.\"\ntraps.8.1.q,\"Complementarity is…\"\ntraps.8.1.options.0,\"a structured pluralism of descriptions (incompatible experimental frameworks)\"\ntraps.8.1.options.1,\"relativism — everyone has their own truth\"\ntraps.8.1.options.2,\"the psychological influence of the observer\"\ntraps.8.1.why,\"Each description is precise and objective within its own conditions.\"\ntraps.8.2.q,\"Does the observer’s consciousness determine the measurement outcome?\"\ntraps.8.2.options.0,\"No — the physical interaction and the experimental arrangement decide.\"\ntraps.8.2.options.1,\"Yes, consciousness creates the outcome.\"\ntraps.8.2.why,\"A role for consciousness does not follow from the quantum formalism.\"\ntraps.8.3.q,\"Which question is EPISTEMOLOGICAL?\"\ntraps.8.3.options.0,\"What can we know about it?\"\ntraps.8.3.options.1,\"What exists?\"\ntraps.8.3.options.2,\"How does the phenomenon appear to us?\"\ntraps.8.3.why,\"Ontology = what exists, phenomenology = how the phenomenon appears.\"\ntraps.8.4.q,\"Bohmian mechanics is…\"\ntraps.8.4.options.0,\"deterministic, with hidden variables, but non-local\"\ntraps.8.4.options.1,\"local and deterministic\"\ntraps.8.4.options.2,\"refuted by Bell’s inequalities\"\ntraps.8.4.why,\"Bell rules out LOCAL hidden variables; Bohm’s are non-local.\"\nl8.kant.lines.0,\"My <i>Critique of Pure Reason</i> says: “The conditions of the possibility of experience are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience.”\"\nl8.kant.lines.1,\"Your quantum mechanics radicalises this: <b>the quantum state represents the conditions of possibility of an outcome</b>, not the outcome itself.\"\nl8.kant.q.q,\"According to the lecture, what does a quantum state represent?\"\nl8.kant.q.options.0,\"the conditions of possibility of an outcome (the structure of possible answers)\"\nl8.kant.q.options.1,\"a hidden list of the values of all quantities\"\nl8.kant.q.options.2,\"the observer’s consciousness\"\nl8.kant.q.why,\"A state is a rule for predictions, not a list of classical properties.\"\nl8.wittg.lines.0,\"“The limits of my language mean the limits of my world.” And later: “The meaning of a word is its use in the language.”\"\nl8.wittg.lines.1,\"Phrases like “spin up” or “measurement along the z axis” are rules of the <b>grammar</b> of physical statements. By the way — I originally studied engineering, aeroplanes.\"\nl8.wittg.q.q,\"According to the lecture, talking about “the value of the spin without a measurement” is…\"\nl8.wittg.q.options.0,\"a violation of the grammar of quantum theory\"\nl8.wittg.q.options.1,\"a deeper ontological description\"\nl8.wittg.q.options.2,\"forbidden by law\"\nl8.wittg.q.why,\"The value of a spin has meaning only in the context of a measurement procedure.\"\nl8.stodola.lines.0,\"Good day — I was born in Liptovský Mikuláš and became a professor at ETH Zürich: steam and gas turbines.\"\nl8.stodola.lines.1,\"I judge theories by whether they can <b>reliably construct</b> relations between causes and effects. Quantum mechanics is a working design framework, not “a picture of the microworld as it is”.\"\nl8.stodola.q.q,\"In quantum engineering, collapse is…\"\nl8.stodola.q.options.0,\"the physical readout process: amplification, coupling to the macroworld, writing to a register\"\nl8.stodola.q.options.1,\"a metaphysical jump without a cause\"\nl8.stodola.q.options.2,\"a consequence of a human looking at the qubit\"\nl8.stodola.q.why,\"It is irreversible and dissipative — an engineering operation, not an extra axiom.\"\nl8.bohm.lines.0,\"I claim that a particle <b>always has a precise position</b> and is guided by a <b>pilot wave</b>. The wave function never collapses. A measurement outcome is revealed, not created.\"\nl8.bohm.lines.1,\"I pay a high price for it: my theory has to be <b>non-local</b>.\"\nl8.bohm.q.q,\"What price does Bohmian mechanics pay for determinism?\"\nl8.bohm.q.options.0,\"non-locality\"\nl8.bohm.q.options.1,\"violation of energy conservation\"\nl8.bohm.q.options.2,\"disagreement with experiment\"\nl8.bohm.q.why,\"Bell’s inequalities rule out LOCAL hidden variables; Bohm’s are non-local.\"\nl8.heis.lines.0,\"On the island of Helgoland in 1925 I found matrix mechanics. In <i>Physics and Philosophy</i> I write: we do not observe nature in itself, but nature exposed to <b>our method of questioning</b>.\"\nl8.heis.lines.1,\"And about uncertainty: [[Δx · Δp ≥ ħ/2]].\"\nl8.heis.q.q,\"What does [[Δx · Δp ≥ ħ/2]] express?\"\nl8.heis.q.options.0,\"a property of the state: position and momentum cannot both be sharply defined\"\nl8.heis.q.options.1,\"only the imprecision of the measuring device\"\nl8.heis.q.options.2,\"Δ = change of position over time\"\nl8.heis.q.why,\"Δ here means the standard deviation (uncertainty) of outcomes on identically prepared systems.\"\nl8.noether.lines.0,\"My theorem: every continuous symmetry has a conservation law. Time → energy, rotation → angular momentum.\"\nl8.noether.lines.1,\"The lecture says: <b>before a measurement the spin exists as a symmetry</b> — a structure of possibilities; <b>after the measurement, as a fact</b>. Symmetry tells you what <i>can</i> happen, not what will happen.\"\nl8.noether.q.q,\"According to the lecture, before a measurement the spin exists as…\"\nl8.noether.q.options.0,\"a symmetry — a structure of possible answers\"\nl8.noether.q.options.1,\"a definite arrow up or down\"\nl8.noether.q.options.2,\"nothing, spin does not exist at all\"\nl8.noether.q.why,\"The value arises only within a concrete experimental framework.\"\nl0.intro.0,\"Welcome to the <b>Hall of Symbols</b> — level 0. Before the mentors start writing equations at you, you will meet their <b>symbols</b>: what each of them means in quantum mechanics.\"\nl0.intro.1,\"Each of the {0} pedestals holds one symbol as a small 3D experiment that moves by itself. Watch <b>what happens</b>: what changes, what stays, and what that means for measurement outcomes.\"\nl0.intro.2,\"After every symbol I will ask you a question, after every chapter there is a review and at the end a big exam. You can turn the camera at any time.\"\nl0.chapter.1,\"<b>Chapter 1 — the state and its amplitudes.</b> What a quantum state is made of and how we ask it questions.\"\nl0.chapter.2,\"<b>Chapter 2 — angles and phases.</b> What changes the probabilities and what only turns the phase.\"\nl0.chapter.3,\"<b>Chapter 3 — operators, probability and the density matrix.</b> What we do to a state and what we measure from it.\"\nl0.chapter.4,\"<b>Chapter 4 — constants, measurement and dynamics.</b> Constants of nature, the collapse in a measurement and the quantities that drive the state’s motion.\"\nl0.review,\"Chapter {0} review: {1} questions on what you have just seen. Feel free to look around the pedestals — they all keep moving.\"\nl0.finale.intro,\"All the pedestals are lit. Now the <b>big exam</b>: {0} questions across the whole hall.\"\nl0.finale.done,\"Hall of Symbols complete! Rating: <b>{0}</b> (mistakes: {1} of {2} questions). Now you know what the symbols in the mentors’ equations mean.\"\nl0.alpha.lines.0,\"This is [[α]] — the <b>amplitude of |0⟩</b>: a complex number the state assigns to the outcome 0. The arrow points to the north pole of the Bloch ball, to |0⟩.\"\nl0.alpha.lines.1,\"The length of the arrow is [[|α|]] and it sets how often 0 comes up: [[P(0) = |α|²]]. The little hand circling the tip is the <b>phase</b> of α — on its own it does not affect P(0).\"\nl0.alpha.q1.q,\"What is α in the state [[|ψ⟩ = α|0⟩ + β|1⟩]]?\"\nl0.alpha.q1.o.0,\"the complex amplitude of the outcome 0\"\nl0.alpha.q1.o.1,\"the probability of the outcome 0\"\nl0.alpha.q1.o.2,\"the tilt angle of the arrow\"\nl0.alpha.q1.why,\"α is an amplitude; the probability is only [[|α|²]].\"\nl0.alpha.q2.q,\"|α| grows from 0.6 to 0.8. What happens to P(0)?\"\nl0.alpha.q2.o.0,\"it grows from 0.36 to 0.64\"\nl0.alpha.q2.o.1,\"it does not change\"\nl0.alpha.q2.o.2,\"it grows from 0.6 to 0.8\"\nl0.alpha.q2.why,\"[[P(0) = |α|²]]: 0.6² = 0.36, 0.8² = 0.64.\"\nl0.beta.lines.0,\"[[β]] is the <b>amplitude of |1⟩</b> — the arrow points down, to the south pole. Together with α it makes up the whole qubit state: [[|ψ⟩ = α|0⟩ + β|1⟩]].\"\nl0.beta.lines.1,\"The probabilities must add up to one: [[|α|² + |β|² = 1]]. When one arrow gets longer, the other must get shorter.\"\nl0.beta.q1.q,\"If [[|α|² = 0.7]], what is [[|β|²]]?\"\nl0.beta.q1.o.0,\"0.3\"\nl0.beta.q1.o.1,\"0.7\"\nl0.beta.q1.o.2,\"0.49\"\nl0.beta.q1.why,\"Normalisation: [[|α|² + |β|² = 1]], so 1 − 0.7 = 0.3.\"\nl0.beta.q2.q,\"Which outcome does β belong to?\"\nl0.beta.q2.o.0,\"the outcome 1 (the state |1⟩, south pole)\"\nl0.beta.q2.o.1,\"the outcome 0 (the state |0⟩)\"\nl0.beta.q2.o.2,\"both equally\"\nl0.beta.q2.why,\"β is the coefficient of |1⟩; [[|β|²]] is the probability of the outcome 1.\"\nl0.ket.lines.0,\"[[|ψ⟩]], [[|0⟩]], [[|1⟩]], [[|+⟩]] are <b>kets</b> — states. A ket is a column of amplitudes: everything that can be predicted about the system.\"\nl0.ket.lines.1,\"|0⟩ and |1⟩ are the poles — a certain outcome when measuring along z. [[|+⟩ = (|0⟩ + |1⟩)/√2]] sits on the equator: along z it gives 0 or 1 with probability ½ each, but along x it gives a certain outcome.\"\nl0.ket.q1.q,\"What does the ket [[|ψ⟩]] describe?\"\nl0.ket.q1.o.0,\"the state of the system — the probabilities of all measurements follow from it\"\nl0.ket.q1.o.1,\"one particular measurement outcome\"\nl0.ket.q1.o.2,\"the position of a particle in space\"\nl0.ket.q1.why,\"A ket is a state: a rule for predictions, not an outcome or a path.\"\nl0.ket.q2.q,\"The state [[|+⟩]] is measured along z. What do we get?\"\nl0.ket.q2.o.0,\"0 or 1, each with probability ½\"\nl0.ket.q2.o.1,\"always 0\"\nl0.ket.q2.o.2,\"always “+”\"\nl0.ket.q2.why,\"Along z, |+⟩ is an even superposition; only a measurement along x gives a certain outcome.\"\nl0.bra.lines.0,\"[[⟨a|]] is a <b>bra</b> — the question “is the system in the state a?”. Mathematically a row: the conjugate transpose of a ket.\"\nl0.bra.lines.1,\"When a bra meets a ket you get <b>one number</b>: [[⟨a|ψ⟩]] is the amplitude to find the state a in a measurement. The probability is [[|⟨a|ψ⟩|²]].\"\nl0.bra.q1.q,\"What is [[⟨a|ψ⟩]]?\"\nl0.bra.q1.o.0,\"one complex number — an amplitude\"\nl0.bra.q1.o.1,\"a new state\"\nl0.bra.q1.o.2,\"an operator\"\nl0.bra.q1.why,\"Bra times ket = a number. The other way round, [[|ψ⟩⟨a|]] would be an operator.\"\nl0.bra.q2.q,\"How do we get the probability of the outcome a from [[⟨a|ψ⟩]]?\"\nl0.bra.q2.o.0,\"[[P(a) = |⟨a|ψ⟩|²]]\"\nl0.bra.q2.o.1,\"[[P(a) = ⟨a|ψ⟩]]\"\nl0.bra.q2.o.2,\"[[P(a) = ⟨ψ|a⟩ + ⟨a|ψ⟩]]\"\nl0.bra.q2.why,\"The Born rule: the squared magnitude of the amplitude.\"\nl0.theta.lines.0,\"[[θ]] is the <b>tilt angle</b> of the state arrow from the north pole |0⟩. At θ = 0 the state is |0⟩, at θ = π it is |1⟩, at θ = π/2 it lies on the equator.\"\nl0.theta.lines.1,\"θ sets the probabilities along z: [[P(0) = cos²(θ/2)]], [[P(1) = sin²(θ/2)]]. Watch the pillars P(0) and P(1) follow the tilt.\"\nl0.theta.q1.q,\"What is P(0) at [[θ = π/2]]?\"\nl0.theta.q1.o.0,\"½\"\nl0.theta.q1.o.1,\"0\"\nl0.theta.q1.o.2,\"1\"\nl0.theta.q1.why,\"[[cos²(π/4) = ½]] — a state on the equator gives 0 and 1 equally often.\"\nl0.theta.q2.q,\"θ grows from 0 to π. What happens to P(1)?\"\nl0.theta.q2.o.0,\"it grows from 0 to 1\"\nl0.theta.q2.o.1,\"nothing — θ is a phase\"\nl0.theta.q2.o.2,\"it falls from 1 to 0\"\nl0.theta.q2.why,\"[[P(1) = sin²(θ/2)]]: 0 at the north pole, 1 at the south pole.\"\nl0.phi.lines.0,\"[[φ]] is the <b>relative phase</b> — the angle of the state arrow about the vertical z axis: [[|ψ⟩ = cos(θ/2)|0⟩ + e<sup>iφ</sup> sin(θ/2)|1⟩]].\"\nl0.phi.lines.1,\"While φ turns, P(0) and P(1) <b>do not change</b>: a measurement along z cannot see the phase. Measurements along x and y do see it, and it decides <b>interference</b>.\"\nl0.phi.q1.q,\"The state arrow turns along the equator (only φ changes). What happens to P(0) and P(1) along z?\"\nl0.phi.q1.o.0,\"nothing — they depend only on θ\"\nl0.phi.q1.o.1,\"P(0) grows\"\nl0.phi.q1.o.2,\"they swap\"\nl0.phi.q1.why,\"φ is a turn about z; the probabilities along z are set by θ alone.\"\nl0.phi.q2.q,\"How do the states [[|+⟩]] and [[|−⟩]] differ?\"\nl0.phi.q2.o.0,\"by a relative phase of π\"\nl0.phi.q2.o.1,\"by the probability P(0)\"\nl0.phi.q2.o.2,\"they don’t — they are the same\"\nl0.phi.q2.why,\"[[|±⟩ = (|0⟩ ± |1⟩)/√2]]: the same P(0) = ½, a different relative phase — a measurement along x tells them apart.\"\nl0.gamma.lines.0,\"[[γ]] is the <b>global phase</b>: a common factor [[e<sup>iγ</sup>]] in front of the whole state. It turns both amplitudes, α and β, by the same angle.\"\nl0.gamma.lines.1,\"The ratios and relative phases of the amplitudes do not change, so [[e<sup>iγ</sup>|ψ⟩]] and [[|ψ⟩]] are <b>physically the same state</b> — no measurement can tell them apart. Look: the pillars do not move.\"\nl0.gamma.q1.q,\"Why can no measurement detect the global phase γ?\"\nl0.gamma.q1.o.0,\"it turns all amplitudes together, nothing relative changes\"\nl0.gamma.q1.o.1,\"it is too small\"\nl0.gamma.q1.o.2,\"it only exists on the island\"\nl0.gamma.q1.why,\"Measurements only see [[|amplitudes|²]] and relative phases; a common [[e<sup>iγ</sup>]] cancels out of them.\"\nl0.gamma.q2.q,\"Can [[|ψ⟩]] and [[−|ψ⟩]] be told apart by a measurement?\"\nl0.gamma.q2.o.0,\"no — they differ only by a global phase π\"\nl0.gamma.q2.o.1,\"yes — they have opposite probabilities\"\nl0.gamma.q2.o.2,\"yes, along x\"\nl0.gamma.q2.why,\"[[−1 = e<sup>iπ</sup>]] in front of the whole state is a global phase.\"\nl0.expi.lines.0,\"[[e<sup>iφ</sup>]] is a <b>phase factor</b>: a complex number of length 1 at the angle φ, [[e<sup>iφ</sup> = cos φ + i sin φ]]. Multiplying an amplitude by it only turns it; the length stays.\"\nl0.expi.lines.1,\"[[i = e<sup>iπ/2</sup>]] is a turn by 90° — watch the small clock click in quarters. Two quarters make half a turn: [[i² = e<sup>iπ</sup> = −1]].\"\nl0.expi.q1.q,\"What does multiplying an amplitude by [[e<sup>iφ</sup>]] do?\"\nl0.expi.q1.o.0,\"turns it by the angle φ, the magnitude stays\"\nl0.expi.q1.o.1,\"stretches it φ times\"\nl0.expi.q1.o.2,\"makes it zero\"\nl0.expi.q1.why,\"[[|e<sup>iφ</sup>| = 1]]: only the phase changes.\"\nl0.expi.q2.q,\"How many times must we multiply 1 by i to get −i?\"\nl0.expi.q2.o.0,\"3 times (270°)\"\nl0.expi.q2.o.1,\"once\"\nl0.expi.q2.o.2,\"4 times\"\nl0.expi.q2.why,\"1 → i → −1 → −i. A fourth multiplication brings us back to 1.\"\nl0.ops.lines.0,\"[[X]], [[Y]], [[Z]], [[H]], [[S]], [[T]] are <b>operators</b> — gates. An operator acts on the state to its right and returns a new state: in [[H|0⟩ = |+⟩]] the gate H turns |0⟩ into |+⟩.\"\nl0.ops.lines.1,\"[[X]] flips |0⟩ ↔ |1⟩, [[Z]] turns the phase (|+⟩ ↔ |−⟩), [[H]] swaps the axes z ↔ x, [[S]] and [[T]] turn about z by 90° and 45°. [[Ĥ]] is the Hamiltonian — the energy that drives the evolution in time; [[Â]] is a measured quantity.\"\nl0.ops.q1.q,\"In [[H|0⟩]], what acts on what?\"\nl0.ops.q1.o.0,\"the gate H acts on the state |0⟩ to its right\"\nl0.ops.q1.o.1,\"|0⟩ acts on H\"\nl0.ops.q1.o.2,\"they multiply like two numbers\"\nl0.ops.q1.why,\"An operator acts to the right: [[H|0⟩ = |+⟩]].\"\nl0.ops.q2.q,\"Which gate swaps the axes z ↔ x (pole ↔ equator)?\"\nl0.ops.q2.o.0,\"H — Hadamard\"\nl0.ops.q2.o.1,\"X\"\nl0.ops.q2.o.2,\"S\"\nl0.ops.q2.o.3,\"I\"\nl0.ops.q2.why,\"H: [[|0⟩ ↔ |+⟩]], [[|1⟩ ↔ |−⟩]]. X flips 0 ↔ 1, S is a quarter turn about z, I does nothing.\"\nl0.P.lines.0,\"[[P]] is the <b>probability</b> of a measurement outcome — a number from 0 to 1 (0 to 100 %). It says how often the outcome comes up over many repetitions.\"\nl0.P.lines.1,\"A probability has <b>no phase</b> any more: it is what measurements actually count. Amplitudes interfere, probabilities are only measured.\"\nl0.P.q1.q,\"What does [[P(0) = 0.5]] mean?\"\nl0.P.q1.o.0,\"over many measurements 0 comes up in about half of the cases\"\nl0.P.q1.o.1,\"the qubit is half in the state 0\"\nl0.P.q1.o.2,\"every measurement gives “half a zero”\"\nl0.P.q1.why,\"Probability is about the statistics of repeated measurements; one measurement always gives a whole outcome.\"\nl0.P.q2.q,\"Which statement is correct?\"\nl0.P.q2.o.0,\"amplitudes interfere; the probability is computed only afterwards\"\nl0.P.q2.o.1,\"probabilities interfere\"\nl0.P.q2.o.2,\"a probability can be negative\"\nl0.P.q2.why,\"[[P = |A₁ + A₂|²]] — amplitudes first, then the square; P is always between 0 and 1.\"\nl0.sq.lines.0,\"[[|α|²]] — the <b>Born rule</b>: the probability is the squared magnitude of the amplitude, [[|α|² = α·α*]].\"\nl0.sq.lines.1,\"Look: under [[|…|²]] the hand stops — <b>the phase cancels</b> and only the length squared remains. A hand of length 0.5 gives [[P = 0.25]].\"\nl0.sq.q1.q,\"What does [[|α|²]] do to the phase of α?\"\nl0.sq.q1.o.0,\"cancels it — a probability carries no phase\"\nl0.sq.q1.o.1,\"doubles it\"\nl0.sq.q1.o.2,\"keeps it\"\nl0.sq.q1.why,\"[[|α|² = α·α*]]: the phases of α and α* cancel each other.\"\nl0.sq.q2.q,\"The amplitude is [[α = 0.6i]]. What is the probability?\"\nl0.sq.q2.o.0,\"0.36\"\nl0.sq.q2.o.1,\"−0.36\"\nl0.sq.q2.o.2,\"0.6\"\nl0.sq.q2.why,\"[[|α|² = 0.6i·(−0.6i) = 0.36]]. A probability is never negative.\"\nl0.rho.lines.0,\"[[ρ]] is the <b>density matrix</b> — a 2 × 2 table that also describes states we do not know completely (mixtures). On the diagonal are the probabilities: [[ρ₀₀ = P(0)]], [[ρ₁₁ = P(1)]].\"\nl0.rho.lines.1,\"Off the diagonal are the <b>coherences</b> [[ρ₀₁]]: they carry the relative phase between |0⟩ and |1⟩. When the environment “measures” the state, the coherences fade — that is <b>decoherence</b>, and interference disappears with it. The diagonal does not change.\"\nl0.rho.q1.q,\"The off-diagonal elements of ρ fade to zero. What is happening?\"\nl0.rho.q1.o.0,\"decoherence — interference is being lost\"\nl0.rho.q1.o.1,\"the probabilities P(0), P(1) change\"\nl0.rho.q1.o.2,\"the state collapses to |0⟩\"\nl0.rho.q1.why,\"The coherences carry the phase relations; their loss is decoherence, the diagonal stays.\"\nl0.rho.q2.q,\"What sits on the diagonal of ρ?\"\nl0.rho.q2.o.0,\"the probabilities of the outcomes 0 and 1\"\nl0.rho.q2.o.1,\"the coherences\"\nl0.rho.q2.o.2,\"the global phase\"\nl0.rho.q2.why,\"[[ρ₀₀ = P(0)]], [[ρ₁₁ = P(1)]]; the coherences are off the diagonal.\"\nl0.const.lines.0,\"[[ħ]] is the <b>reduced Planck constant</b>, [[ħ = h/2π]]: the scale of the quantum world — an electron’s spin has the projection ±ħ/2. [[π]] is half a turn (180°): from |0⟩ to |1⟩ on the Bloch ball.\"\nl0.const.lines.1,\"[[Σ]] means a sum, [[∂/∂t]] the rate of change in time, and [[⊗]] the <b>tensor product</b>: it joins systems, e.g. [[|0⟩ ⊗ |1⟩ = |01⟩]] — the dimensions multiply.\"\nl0.const.q1.q,\"What spin projection do we measure on an electron in the Stern–Gerlach experiment?\"\nl0.const.q1.o.0,\"+ħ/2 or −ħ/2\"\nl0.const.q1.o.1,\"any value from −ħ to +ħ\"\nl0.const.q1.o.2,\"always 0\"\nl0.const.q1.why,\"Spin ½ has only two values along a chosen direction: ±ħ/2.\"\nl0.const.q2.q,\"Two qubits joined by the tensor product ⊗ — how many amplitudes does their state have?\"\nl0.const.q2.o.0,\"4 (2 × 2)\"\nl0.const.q2.o.1,\"2 (2 + 2)\"\nl0.const.q2.o.2,\"1\"\nl0.const.q2.why,\"[[⊗]] multiplies the dimensions: |00⟩, |01⟩, |10⟩, |11⟩.\"\nl0.collapse.lines.0,\"<b>Measurement</b>: before it the state has two terms, |0⟩ with the amplitude α and |1⟩ with the amplitude β. A measurement gives <b>one</b> outcome — 0 with probability [[|α|²]], 1 with probability [[|β|²]].\"\nl0.collapse.lines.1,\"After the measurement the state is entirely the measured outcome (<b>collapse</b>): the other term is gone and measuring again gives the same. The original superposition and its phase are lost.\"\nl0.collapse.q1.q,\"The state [[(|0⟩ + |1⟩)/√2]] is measured and we get 1. What does the next measurement along z give?\"\nl0.collapse.q1.o.0,\"always 1\"\nl0.collapse.q1.o.1,\"0 or 1, 50 % each\"\nl0.collapse.q1.o.2,\"always 0\"\nl0.collapse.q1.why,\"After the measurement the state is |1⟩; repeating the measurement along the same axis gives a certain outcome.\"\nl0.collapse.q2.q,\"What is lost in a measurement along z?\"\nl0.collapse.q2.o.0,\"the superposition and the relative phase of the original state\"\nl0.collapse.q2.o.1,\"nothing — the state stays the same\"\nl0.collapse.q2.o.2,\"only the global phase\"\nl0.collapse.q2.why,\"The collapse picks one term; the information about the phase between |0⟩ and |1⟩ is gone.\"\nl0.srn.lines.0,\"[[σ]] are the <b>Pauli matrices</b> σ<sub>x</sub>, σ<sub>y</sub>, σ<sub>z</sub> — spin along three axes at once. [[r]] is the <b>Bloch vector</b>: [[r = (⟨X⟩, ⟨Y⟩, ⟨Z⟩)]]; length 1 = a pure state, shorter = a mixture.\"\nl0.srn.lines.1,\"[[n]] is the <b>measurement direction</b> — the axis we ask about. The probability of the outcome “+” is [[P(+n) = ½(1 + r·n)]]: the more r points along n, the higher it is.\"\nl0.srn.q1.q,\"The Bloch vector r shrinks into the ball. What does that mean?\"\nl0.srn.q1.o.0,\"the state is becoming a mixture\"\nl0.srn.q1.o.1,\"the state is turning into |0⟩\"\nl0.srn.q1.o.2,\"a gate acted\"\nl0.srn.q1.why,\"[[|r| = 1]] is a pure state on the surface, [[|r| < 1]] a mixture inside, the centre is fully mixed.\"\nl0.srn.q2.q,\"The state points exactly along the measurement axis n (r = n). What is P(+n)?\"\nl0.srn.q2.o.0,\"1\"\nl0.srn.q2.o.1,\"½\"\nl0.srn.q2.o.2,\"0\"\nl0.srn.q2.why,\"[[P(+n) = ½(1 + r·n) = ½(1 + 1) = 1]].\"\nl0.nmr.lines.0,\"[[ω]] is the <b>angular frequency of precession</b>: in a magnetic field the spin arrow circles about the z axis, [[ω₀ = γB₀]]. Precession changes only the phase φ; the probabilities along z stay.\"\nl0.nmr.lines.1,\"[[Ω]] (the Rabi frequency) sets how fast a resonant pulse <b>changes the tilt θ</b>. [[Δ]] is the <b>detuning</b> — how far the radio frequency is from ω₀; with Δ ≠ 0 the spin does not flip completely.\"\nl0.nmr.q1.q,\"What does precession at the frequency ω₀ do?\"\nl0.nmr.q1.o.0,\"turns the phase φ about z; P(0) and P(1) do not change\"\nl0.nmr.q1.o.1,\"flips the spin from |0⟩ to |1⟩\"\nl0.nmr.q1.o.2,\"shrinks the Bloch vector\"\nl0.nmr.q1.why,\"Precession is a rotation about z — it changes only the relative phase.\"\nl0.nmr.q2.q,\"What does the Rabi frequency Ω set?\"\nl0.nmr.q2.o.0,\"how fast the tilt θ changes during a pulse\"\nl0.nmr.q2.o.1,\"the precession speed without a pulse\"\nl0.nmr.q2.o.2,\"the strength of decoherence\"\nl0.nmr.q2.why,\"At resonance θ grows at the rate Ω; a π pulse flips |0⟩ to |1⟩.\"\nl0.ptA.lines.0,\"[[p]] is the <b>strength of decoherence</b>: how much phase information the environment carries away. The coherence shrinks to [[(1 − p)ρ₀₁]]; at p = 1 the state is a full mixture in the z basis. [[t]] is time.\"\nl0.ptA.lines.1,\"[[A]] is a <b>path amplitude</b>: every indistinguishable path to an outcome carries its own amplitude. They are added first and only then squared: [[P = |A₁ + A₂|²]]. When they point against each other, the outcome never happens.\"\nl0.ptA.q1.q,\"Two paths with the amplitudes [[A₁ = ½]] and [[A₂ = −½]]. What is the probability of the outcome?\"\nl0.ptA.q1.o.0,\"0 — the amplitudes cancel\"\nl0.ptA.q1.o.1,\"½\"\nl0.ptA.q1.o.2,\"¼\"\nl0.ptA.q1.why,\"[[P = |½ − ½|² = 0]]: destructive interference.\"\nl0.ptA.q2.q,\"What does strong decoherence (p → 1) do in an interference experiment?\"\nl0.ptA.q2.o.0,\"interference disappears; probabilities simply add\"\nl0.ptA.q2.o.1,\"interference gets stronger\"\nl0.ptA.q2.o.2,\"nothing\"\nl0.ptA.q2.why,\"Without coherences there is no interference term: [[P = |A₁|² + |A₂|²]].\"\nl0.final.1.q,\"Which notation is a state (a ket)?\"\nl0.final.1.o.0,\"[[|ψ⟩]]\"\nl0.final.1.o.1,\"[[⟨ψ|]]\"\nl0.final.1.o.2,\"[[|ψ⟩⟨ψ|]]\"\nl0.final.1.why,\"The ket |ψ⟩ is a state; ⟨ψ| is a bra (a question), |ψ⟩⟨ψ| is an operator (a projector).\"\nl0.final.2.q,\"Which of these is a number?\"\nl0.final.2.o.0,\"[[⟨ψ|ψ⟩]]\"\nl0.final.2.o.1,\"[[|ψ⟩⟨ψ|]]\"\nl0.final.2.o.2,\"[[Ĥ|ψ⟩]]\"\nl0.final.2.why,\"Bra times ket = a number (here 1). |ψ⟩⟨ψ| is an operator, Ĥ|ψ⟩ is a state.\"\nl0.final.3.q,\"Which angle changes the probabilities along z?\"\nl0.final.3.o.0,\"θ — the tilt from the pole\"\nl0.final.3.o.1,\"φ — the relative phase\"\nl0.final.3.o.2,\"γ — the global phase\"\nl0.final.3.why,\"[[P(0) = cos²(θ/2)]]; φ and γ are phases.\"\nl0.final.4.q,\"Which phase can no measurement see?\"\nl0.final.4.o.0,\"the global phase γ\"\nl0.final.4.o.1,\"the relative phase φ\"\nl0.final.4.o.2,\"none — every phase can be measured\"\nl0.final.4.why,\"The global phase cancels out of all probabilities; the relative phase is seen by a measurement along x or y.\"\nl0.final.5.q,\"How do we compute the probability for two indistinguishable paths?\"\nl0.final.5.o.0,\"[[P = |A₁ + A₂|²]] — add the amplitudes first\"\nl0.final.5.o.1,\"[[P = |A₁|² + |A₂|²]]\"\nl0.final.5.o.2,\"[[P = A₁ + A₂]]\"\nl0.final.5.why,\"Amplitudes interfere; the probability is only the square of their sum.\"\nl0.final.6.q,\"What does the gate X do to the state |0⟩?\"\nl0.final.6.o.0,\"turns it into |1⟩\"\nl0.final.6.o.1,\"turns it into |+⟩\"\nl0.final.6.o.2,\"leaves it unchanged\"\nl0.final.6.why,\"X is a 180° turn about the x axis: [[X|0⟩ = |1⟩]].\"\nl0.final.7.q,\"The coherences in ρ fade while the diagonal stays. The state…\"\nl0.final.7.o.0,\"loses interference but keeps its probabilities along z\"\nl0.final.7.o.1,\"collapses to |1⟩\"\nl0.final.7.o.2,\"gains energy\"\nl0.final.7.why,\"Decoherence changes only the off-diagonal of ρ.\"\nl0.final.8.q,\"Which Bloch vector belongs to a fully mixed state?\"\nl0.final.8.o.0,\"[[r = 0]] (the centre of the ball)\"\nl0.final.8.o.1,\"[[|r| = 1]] (the surface)\"\nl0.final.8.o.2,\"r pointing to |0⟩\"\nl0.final.8.why,\"A full mixture is [[ρ = I/2]] — a Bloch vector of zero length.\"\nl0.final.9.q,\"What does the Hamiltonian Ĥ describe?\"\nl0.final.9.o.0,\"the energy of the system — it drives the evolution in time\"\nl0.final.9.o.1,\"one particular gate\"\nl0.final.9.o.2,\"the probability of an outcome\"\nl0.final.9.why,\"The Schrödinger equation: [[iħ ∂|ψ⟩/∂t = Ĥ|ψ⟩]].\"\nl0.final.10.q,\"How many amplitudes does the state of three qubits have?\"\nl0.final.10.o.0,\"8\"\nl0.final.10.o.1,\"6\"\nl0.final.10.o.2,\"3\"\nl0.final.10.why,\"[[2 ⊗ 2 ⊗ 2]]: the dimensions multiply, 2³ = 8.\"\n");
